You take a deep breath of salty air as the first raindrops begin to spatter the pavement, and the swollen, slate-colored clouds that blanket the sky mutter ominous portents amongst themselves over the little coastal town of Anchorhead. Squinting up into the storm, you wonder how everything managed to happen so fast. The strange phone call from a lawyer claiming to represent the estate of some distant branch of Michael's family was only a month ago. Then came the sudden whirlwind of planning and decisions, legal details and travel arrangements, the packing up and shipping away of your entire life . . . And now here you are, over a thousand miles from home after driving for two days straight, getting ready to move into the ancestral mansion of a clan of relatives so far removed that not even Michael has ever heard of them. And you've only been married since June and none of this was any of your idea in the first place, and already it's starting to rain. A sullen belch emanates from the clouds, and the rain starts coming down harder — fat, cold drops smacking loudly against the cobblestones. Shouldn't it be snowing in New England at this time of year? With a sigh, you open your umbrella. Welcome to Anchorhead . . . THE FIRST DAY I was far from home, and the spell of the eastern sea was upon me. — H. P. Lovecraft, "The Festival" Anchorhead an interactive Lovecraftian gothic by Michael Gentry Release 1 / Serial number 171017 / Inform 7 build 6M62 (I6/v6.33 lib 6/12N) Illustrated Edition Type ABOUT for more information about this game. Outside the Real Estate Office A grim little cul-de-sac, tucked away in one corner of the claustrophobic tangle of narrow, twisting avenues that comprise the older portion of Anchorhead. Like most of the streets in this city, it is ancient, shadowy, and leads essentially nowhere. The lane ends here at the real estate agent's office, which lies to the east, and winds its way back towards the center of town to the west. A narrow, garbage-choked alley opens to the southeast. You shake yourself and try to force the melancholy thoughts from your head. You're supposed to meet with the real estate agent and get the keys to your new house, while Michael runs across town to take care of some paperwork at the university. He'll be back to pick you up in a few minutes, and then the two of you can begin the long, precarious process of settling in. You rap on the glass sharply, peering through it into the dark room inside. No one answers. Strange; you just talked to the real estate agent yesterday. She was supposed to meet you here. Garbage-Choked Alley This narrow aperture between two buildings is choked with piles of rotting cardboard boxes and overstuffed garbage cans. Ugly, half-crumbling brick walls totter over you on either side. The east end of the alley is blocked by a tall, wooden fence. A rusty fire escape clings to the side of the northern building. The ladder is retracted, its lowest rung hanging about ten feet up, above one of the garbage cans. You clamber onto the wobbling garbage can, precariously balanced. The bottom rung of the fire escape is now just a few feet above your head. (first closing the umbrella) You hook the handle of your umbrella around the bottom rung and put your whole weight on it. With a sudden, ratcheting crash that nearly knocks you off the garbage can, the ladder slides down and locks into place. You are getting wet. The entire fire escape rattles ominously as you shimmy up the ladder. Fire Escape A shaky metal platform bolted to crumbling brick, about fifteen feet up. There is a window here at waist height, big enough for you to crawl through. A metal ladder provides access to the alley below. In the distance, you can hear the lonesome keening of a train whistle drifting on the wind. You are getting wet. For a moment you are sure it will be locked, but to your surprise the window slides up easily. You hesitate. Breaking and entering is not your preferred way to spend a rainy afternoon, but the real estate agent was supposed to be here and dammit, you need those keys. Surely the judge will show leniency once you explain everything. With a sigh, you slip in through the window. File Room Peering through the murk, you can make out the blocky outlines of filing cabinets lining the walls. A narrow stairway leads down to the office proper, and a window looks out onto a fire escape to the south. Office Grayish light trickles in through the drawn blinds. The office is quiet, deserted. A narrow staircase in the back leads up to the file room, and a glass-paneled door leads west to the street. Sitting on the corner of the paper-strewn desk are a telephone and an answering machine. Someone has left a cup of coffee out, unfinished and cold. You hear a fly buzzing around somewhere nearby. For a while there is nothing but a quiet hiss, followed by intermittent skirls of static. It sounds like one of those annoying glitches where the caller hangs up but the machine keeps recording anyway. Then, barely audible through the static, you detect what sounds like a human voice whispering a single word: "Verlac." The machine beeps. A shudder ripples up your back. You remember now, "Verlac" is the name of this branch of Michael's family. File Room Peering through the murk, you can make out the blocky outlines of filing cabinets lining the walls. A narrow stairway leads down to the office proper, and a window looks out onto a fire escape to the south. You find the file for the Verlac property easily enough — but the file is empty. Title, deed, all the papers, all of it gone. In the back of the drawer, however, is a set of keys that must have slipped out of the hanging folder. Relieved, you pocket them. Office Grayish light trickles in through the drawn blinds. The office is quiet, deserted. A narrow staircase in the back leads up to the file room, and a glass-paneled door leads west to the street. Sitting on the corner of the paper-strewn desk are a telephone and an answering machine. Someone has left a cup of coffee out, unfinished and cold. You turn the latch, unlocking the glass-paneled door. You open the glass-paneled door. The rain is still coming down, so you open your umbrella. Outside the Real Estate Office A grim little cul-de-sac, tucked away in one corner of the claustrophobic tangle of narrow, twisting avenues that comprise the older portion of Anchorhead. Like most of the streets in this city, it is ancient, shadowy, and leads essentially nowhere. The lane ends here at the real estate agent's office, which lies to the east, and winds its way back towards the center of town to the west. A narrow, garbage-choked alley opens to the southeast. Narrow Street As the street winds along from east to west, it narrows until the rooftops on either side practically touch each other. To the south is a twisting lane even narrower than this one, while a short flight of steps to the north leads down to the local watering hole. Overhead, the clouds flicker in a greenish haze of sheet lightning. North Square To the north, a gap in the crowded press of buildings opens onto a road heading out over a grassy heath. The main street continues to the south towards Whateley Bridge and the center of town, while a narrow side street leads east. At the top of a steep drive to the northwest, you can just make out the ivy-covered walls of the university. University Courtyard The university campus huddles around this tiny, cobbled court, isolated and serene behind ivy-covered walls. City streets lie southeast. To the west is the library, where Michael told you he'd be until he came back to the real estate agent's office to pick you up — which, you cannot help but note with some annoyance, he has not yet done. You recall Michael describing this place on the long drive up here. Founded in the late 1800s, the university represents this benighted town's single, if dubious, claim to cultural achievement. The school's reputation and enrollment have diminished over the years as Anchorhead drifts further and further into the abyss of provincial backwaterism, but the library, at least, is still recognized for its collection of folklore and esoteric mythology. And the Board of Deans was kind enough to offer your husband a tenured position upon hearing of his recently discovered heritage and his plans to move into the estate. Not exactly Ivy League, but they take do care of their own. The rain beats down ceaselessly. You close your umbrella as you step inside. Reading Room Most of the front area is taken up by reading desks, their green-shaded lamps casting tiny islands of radiance here and there amidst the library's dim interior. Shadows roost beneath the high, vaulted ceiling, and whispers rustle at the edge of your hearing. An exit lies east, and a small alcove to the north houses the circulation desk. Peering through the dimness, you spot your husband at one of the reading desks, absorbed in some sort of weighty tome and clearly oblivious to the time. Michael looks up, startled, then realizes it's you. "Hi hon," he says. He closes the book and stretches. "Have you been waiting a long time? I'm so sorry — I just got absorbed here and lost track of time." He smiles sheepishly. "Well?" he asks. "Did you get the keys?" "Great! Let's see 'em." (first taking the keyring from your pocket) "Excellent!" says Michael. "You hold on to that, I'll go put this book away." Michael goes north. Time passes. Time passes. Michael arrives from the north. Michael suddenly smacks his forehead. "Oh, crap, I forgot to tell you. The car broke down." He spreads his hands. "I don't know what happened to it; one second I'm parking outside, the next second it dies and I can't get it started again. I called a tow truck, but the nearest garage is over in Innswich. They took the car but won't be able to look at it until next week at the earliest." He offers you a lame smile. "I guess we're walking home. You can hold the umbrella." The rain is still coming down, so you open your umbrella. University Courtyard The university campus huddles around this tiny, cobbled court, isolated and serene behind ivy-covered walls. City streets lie southeast. To the west is the library. Michael follows you. North Square To the north, a gap in the crowded press of buildings opens onto a road heading out over a grassy heath. The main street continues to the south towards Whateley Bridge and the center of town, while a narrow side street leads east. At the top of a steep drive to the northwest, you can just make out the ivy-covered walls of the university. Michael hurries to catch up. "Wait," says Michael. "I think it's the other way." Overhead, the clouds flicker in a greenish haze of sheet lightning. "No," says Michael, putting his hand on your arm. "We need to get to the house. I'm pretty sure it isn't that way." "I apologized about the car, right?" says Michael. Whateley Bridge A hoary monument of crumbling, moss-eaten flagstones, Whateley Bridge is possibly older than any other structure in the entire town. It spans the dark Miskahawnee River, connecting the north and south halves of the city and occasionally raining bits of mortar from its underside into the torpid water. The rain beats down ceaselessly. Michael strolls along after you. Town Square The claustrophobic streets finally open out here, onto this wide expanse of uneven pavestones in the center of town. The municipal courthouse stands at the south end of the square. Avenues to the west and east lead back into the cramped, ingrown streets, while to the north lies Whateley Bridge. In the center of the square, rising from a circular lawn of unhealthy-looking grass, stands a stone obelisk. "Wow," Michael says, looking the obelisk up and down. "That is, uh . . . that is an impressive monument. I wonder what it's for?" Shadowy Corner The buildings on this side of town are taller, and thus block more of the sky, making this neighborhood a particularly dark and unpleasant one. The main street leads away to the east, and a shadowy driveway leads through a high brick wall to the southwest. The clouds overhead mutter restlessly to themselves. Michael hurries to catch up. Asylum Courtyard This tiny, shadowed court is dominated by the grim edifice of Dalmers Asylum to the west. A driveway to the northeast leads back to the street, and a narrow alley leads to the east. Michael stops and stares up at the stark edifice of the asylum. "Dalmers," he murmurs. "This is where they put Edward, after . . ." He takes a deep breath. "He killed himself here. Bashed his brains out against the wall of his cell, according to what I read." Then he shakes himself. "Huh. Creepy place. Come on, let's get out of here." He smiles at you, but for a moment he almost looks pale. Shadowy Corner The buildings on this side of town are taller, and thus block more of the sky, making this neighborhood a particularly dark and unpleasant one. The main street leads away to the east, and a shadowy driveway leads through a high brick wall to the southwest. Michael follows you. Town Square The claustrophobic streets open out here, onto this wide expanse of uneven pavestones in the center of town. The municipal courthouse stands at the south end of the square. Avenues to the west and east lead back into the cramped, ingrown streets, while to the north lies Whateley Bridge. In the center of the square, rising from a circular lawn of unhealthy-looking grass, stands a stone obelisk. Michael strolls along after you. Riverwalk This street follows along the mud-slick banks of the Miskahawnee before bending south, turning into a misty avenue between the trees. Steep, stone steps lead down to the water's edge. The town square lies west, and a vacant lot lies east. A sudden gust blows a cold spray of rain into your face. Michael follows you. Chilly Avenue It is noticeably cooler along this lonely street at the edge of town, perhaps due to some unwholesome vapour rising from the waters of the nearby Miskahawnee. Dirt roads lead west and south, into the dense woods at the edge of town. A clammy mist hangs in the air, seeping through your clothes and making you shiver. Michael hurries to catch up, but stops when he sees the mist. He stares at it for some time. "It's strange," he murmurs, "I feel like I've seen this before." He takes a step forward and is soon standing in the midst of the curling vapours. Suddenly, the mist parts before him and dissolves away into nothing. Michael hardly seems surprised at all. Michael heads west, quickening his pace. The road climbs sharply, heading up into the hills. Scenic View Halfway up the hill, a break in the trees reveals a panoramic view of the Miskahawnee River Valley and the squalid little town of Anchorhead nestled within it. The town is a jumbled sprawl of rooftops spread out below you. You can see the university almost directly to the north and, a bit further out, the smokestacks of the paper mill. The solitary lighthouse and surrounding ocean are visible to the northeast, and to the east you can see the shattered steeple of an old church rising above the treetops. Winding through it all is the thin black ribbon of the Miskahawnee, and right in the center lies the little clearing of Town Square. You can just make out the shape of the obelisk from up here. The road doubles back on itself, east to southeast, winding its tortuous way up to the top of the hill. Michael is here. Michael goes southeast. Front of the House Gnarled and ancient trees surround a small clearing at the top of the hill. The road enters the clearing from the northwest and sweeps around in a wide curve, ending in front of the house. It looms before you in the deepening twilight, the fabled Verlac family mansion. Its dark, creaking presence dominates the clearing and, somehow, even though it is not visible through the trees, the entire valley below. Somehow, the shadow of the Verlacs seems to enshroud all of Anchorhead from here. Attached to the front door is a typewritten notice. Michael waits impatiently by the front door. You pull the notice down and squint at it in the gloom. It's a letter from the Miskahawnee Regional Utilities Company, explaining that, due to wiring difficulties, the electricity will not be installed until next week. No phone service, either. "Well, that's wonderful news," remarks Michael dryly, reading over your shoulder. You unlock the front door with the key to the house. You open the front door. Overhead, the clouds flicker in a greenish haze of sheet lightning. Michael enters the house. You close your umbrella as you step inside. Foyer A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south into pitch-black rooms, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north the front door stands wide open. Stacked in a towering heap in the middle of the room are all your luggage and belongings, which you had sent ahead through a moving company before driving up to Massachusetts. The reality of this move finally slams home as you stare at the jumble of stuff, virtually everything you own and care about, boxed away and piled up in the middle of the floor. Michael is here. Night has fallen. A cold, noiseless draft coils through the foyer and up your back. You are momentarily seized with an almost childlike fear: that of being tiny, timid, and alone in a big, dark place. The feeling passes, though not without a shiver. Michael stretches his arms and yawns. "Well," he says, "I think I'm going to turn in. There'll be plenty of time to unpack and explore tomorrow. Good night, hon." He kisses you on the cheek. "Don't stay up too late." Michael goes upstairs. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Michael is here. Michael goes south. Master Bedroom Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east. Michael is here. Michael crawls into bed, pulls a huge swath of covers around himself, and falls to sleep almost instantly. You take off your clothes, leave your things on the floor, crawl into bed next to Michael and pull the covers up to your chin. You toss and turn for a few minutes, plagued by the nagging sensation that you've forgotten something. Suddenly you remember — you've left the front door open and unlocked. Michael rolls over, then sinks back into troubled sleep. (first reluctantly getting out of bed) (first taking your clothes) You put on your clothes. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Foyer A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south into pitch-black rooms, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north the front door stands wide open. Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer. You close the front door. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Master Bedroom Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east. Michael is curled up in bed, fast asleep. You can see your umbrella, a keyring with a couple of keys on it, a typewritten notice, and your trenchcoat here. Taken. Taken. Taken. You put on the trenchcoat. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Foyer A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south into pitch-black rooms, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north is the front door. Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer. On the keyring are the key to the house and the key to the cellar. You lock the front door. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Master Bedroom Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east. Michael is curled up in bed, fast asleep. You can see a typewritten notice here. You take off your clothes, leave your things on the floor, crawl into bed next to Michael and pull the covers up to your chin. Almost immediately you fall asleep, and as the wind slithers around the eaves and the house creaks furtive secrets to itself, you drift into troubling dreams . . . You are waiting in your bed, with the coverlet up under your chin, waiting for Father to come tuck you in. Footsteps in the hall pause outside your door. You close your eyes; when you open them again Father is standing at the foot of the bed. His eyes are bloodshot, rimmed with red. You wonder if he has been crying. Slowly, he starts to take off his belt. Only it's not your father after all, it's Michael. He hands you a golden sphere and says: "I don't know what it opens yet but I have an idea." Your reflection is like a little doll trapped inside the sphere. Everything is tinged with gold. Your face swells and stretches comically as you stare back at yourself through the curved, distorting surface. Reaching out, your fingers curve back on themselves. The sphere shatters . . . You are running from the eyes, the terrible red-rimmed eyes, through endless, narrow corridors of wooden slats and crumbling plaster. You stumble through turn after turn, hopelessly lost. There is a small hole in the boards, and peering through it you see Michael in the study, typing diligently away. You scream, you pound on the wall, but he can't hear you. The eyes draw closer. You hurry down a twisting passage until you stop abruptly at a dead end. The walls crumble . . . You are running through the streets, following the wind, chasing something terribly important, until you reach another dead end, a high brick wall at the center of a labyrinth of twisting lanes. Written across the wall in black spray paint are the words: He always returns to his blood. Behind you, you hear the buzzing of a fly. You turn . . . THE SECOND DAY It is hard to prevent the impression of a faint, malign odour about the village street, as of the massed mould and decay of centuries . . . It is always a relief to get clear of the place. — H. P. Lovecraft, "The Dunwich Horror" You wake up. Steam billows gently by, and you can hear the sound of running water coming from the bathroom. Master Bedroom (on the four-poster bed) Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east. Clouds of steam billow out from the direction of the bathroom. Your clothes lie in an untidy pile on the floor. Michael's pants are draped across the foot of the bed. You can also see a keyring with a couple of keys on it, your umbrella, your trenchcoat, and a typewritten notice here. You overhear an odd noise coming from the bathroom . . . it almost sounds likesome sort of . . . crying? Reluctantly, you do so. You overhear Michael having what sounds like a heated argument with himself. (first taking your clothes) You put on your clothes. Taken. You can hear Michael singing, as he often does. Taken. Taken. You put on the trenchcoat. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. The shower suddenly stops. Foyer A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north is the front door. Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer. Dining Room A huge dining table runs the length of the room, its surface hidden under a thick blanket of dust. Above it, a crystal chandelier twinkles weakly in the gloom. Doorways lead elsewhere to the south and west. Kitchen The kitchen has been picked completely clean; nothing but ranks of dusty cabinets and empty hooks line the walls. Doorways lead west and north, a small pantry lies to the east, and to the south, the back door leads out of the house. Someone's flashlight is lying on the kitchen counter; one of the movers must have left it. An odd feeling of gloom overtakes you momentarily, and you find yourself thinking about the Verlac family's personal effects — the everyday mundanities, the spare lightbulbs, the kitchen utensils. Were they all thrown out? Donated to charity? Auctioned off as grisly souvenirs? For a morbid minute or two, you are fascinated by the question of what Mrs. Edward Verlac might have been cooking for dinner, the night her husband blew her head off. What about canned goods, did anyone think to take them? Could they still be in the cabinets? You pick up the flashlight. It's the kind where the top swivels, so you can put it in your pocket and it will still shine out and illuminate your way. Looks waterproof, too. It is switched off. Pantry The pantry is empty save for the dust on the shelves. A narrow door in the back presumably leads down to the cellar. You can return to the kitchen to the west. Leaning in the corner is an old broom. You pick up the broom. You unlock the cellar door with the key to the cellar. You open the cellar door. You descend the steps into the dank, cave-like cellar. Darkness It is pitch dark, and you can't see a thing. The flashlight emits a warm yellow beam. Cellar Moisture gleams on the old stone walls; sagging timbers creak uneasily above your head. Portions of the cellar extend north and west into the clammy darkness, while stairs lead back up to the ground floor. A thick bundle of wires leads to a rusty old fuse cabinet on the wall. Storage Room Old crates and boxes piled high against the walls make this room seem even smaller and dingier than it actually is, which is saying a lot. A doorway lies south; the other walls contain nothing but shadows and dirt-filled corners. You spend some time poking dispiritedly through boxes of second-hand table settings, discarded shoes, and outdated encyclopedias, but the most interesting things you manage to turn up are an old fuse and a big, nasty-looking spider web in a corner behind one of the crates. With a single sweep of the broom, you clear the spider web away, revealing an old-fashioned iron key lying in the corner. (slipping the flashlight into your pocket to make room, since your hands are now full) You pick up the old-fashioned iron key. It's an antique key, the kind with a long shaft and a big, square bit at the end. Cellar Moisture gleams on the old stone walls; sagging timbers creak uneasily above your head. Portions of the cellar extend north and west into the clammy darkness, while stairs lead back up to the ground floor. A thick bundle of wires leads to a rusty old fuse cabinet on the wall. Wine Cellar This wing of the cellar looks like it was part of an earlier structure, older than the rest of the house, with rough walls made of unmortared stone. Tall, heavy wine racks lean in on all sides — all of them empty. An exit to the east leads back to the main cellar. You are reminded of something Michael told you during the long drive up: about how when the police arrived after the shooting, they found the wine cellar in a shambles. Every bottle thrown to the floor and shattered, the whole cellar reeking of rotted grapes. Every single bottle. No one could posit a motive for it. The racks must have at one time held hundreds and hundreds of bottles; now the notched shelves are empty. Behind one of the racks, you can make out some markings on the wall. Cellar Moisture gleams on the old stone walls; sagging timbers creak uneasily above your head. Portions of the cellar extend north and west into the clammy darkness, while stairs lead back up to the ground floor. A thick bundle of wires leads to a rusty old fuse cabinet on the wall. Pantry The pantry is empty save for the dust on the shelves. A narrow door in the back stands open, revealing dark stairs leading down to the cellar. You can return to the kitchen to the west. Cellar Moisture gleams on the old stone walls; sagging timbers creak uneasily above your head. Portions of the cellar extend north and west into the clammy darkness, while stairs lead back up to the ground floor. A thick bundle of wires leads to a rusty old fuse cabinet on the wall. Wine Cellar This wing of the cellar looks like it was part of an earlier structure, older than the rest of the house, with rough walls made of unmortared stone. Tall, heavy wine racks lean in on all sides — all of them empty. An exit to the east leads back to the main cellar. You can't get a good look at the markings with the wine rack in the way. You manage to drag the wine rack over to one side, revealing a mural covering the north wall. The entire north wall is covered with a bas-relief mural depicting . . . you're not sure what, exactly. Groups of raised bumps scattered randomly here and there, intersecting lines and arcs dividing the space into irregularly shaped sections, circles of various sizes. The entire north wall is covered with a bas-relief mural depicting . . . you're not sure what, exactly. Groups of raised bumps scattered randomly here and there, intersecting lines and arcs dividing the space into irregularly shaped sections, circles of various sizes. Cellar Moisture gleams on the old stone walls; sagging timbers creak uneasily above your head. Portions of the cellar extend north and west into the clammy darkness, while stairs lead back up to the ground floor. A thick bundle of wires leads to a rusty old fuse cabinet on the wall. Pantry The pantry is empty save for the dust on the shelves. A narrow door in the back stands open, revealing dark stairs leading down to the cellar. You can return to the kitchen to the west. Kitchen The kitchen has been picked completely clean; nothing but ranks of dusty cabinets and empty hooks line the walls. Doorways lead west and north, a small pantry lies to the east, and to the south, the back door leads out of the house. You switch the flashlight off. Back Hall A short hallway, connecting rooms to the east, west and north. Foyer A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north is the front door. Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer. Sitting Room A sparse but functional parlor. An old, colonial rug covers the floor, and the sofa is arranged so that visitors can enjoy the warmth of the large, ornate fireplace that fills the southern wall. Rooms lie to the east and south. On the antique sofa is a scrapbook. From above the fireplace, a massive portrait glares down at you with morbid contempt. The man in the portrait is the very epitome of Puritan patriarchy, from the tarnished buckle of his hat to the grimly knotted bows of his garters. Though his outward pose seems dignified, you think you detect the unsettling glow of fanaticism in his haggard, red-rimmed eyes: a hint of something cruel, possibly even insane. If this was the original founder of the Verlac clan, perhaps it's no wonder they all ended up murdered or mad. (slipping the old-fashioned iron key into your pocket to make room) You pick up the scrapbook. It's just a cheap scrapbook album, the kind you can get in any craft store. The first dozen or more pages are pasted over with yellowed documents and newspaper clippings. Among the clippings you find: - a reproduction of a woodcut illustration - a photostatic copy - an article dated 1919 - an article dated 1935 - an article dated 1952 - an article dated 1956 - a mimeographed bulletin - an obituary - an article dated 1960 - an article dated 1973 - an article dated 1988 - a crumpled flyer - a wedding announcement (a reproduction of an antique woodcut illustration) The old, sepia-toned illustration depicts some sort of astrological event in medieval times. A comet hangs darkly in the sky, its tail reaching down towards the earth like a cluster of grasping tendrils. Buildings crumble and burn in the town below. Townsfolk scurry and clutch their heads in despair . . . or are they dancing? Shouting and reveling in joy? The more you look, the harder it is to tell. (a photostatic copy of a much older document; a note in the margin dates it to the end of the 18th century) BE IT KNOWNE that, through the Generoſitie and Enterpriſe of the Hon. Goodman Heinrich Verlac, the Miſkahawnee Valley Mille shall be Rebuilt, even upon the Ashes of the Olde which was Tragickally Burnt; and BE IT KNOWNE that, upon its Newe Christening, there shall Ariſe a Sore Need for Men of Able Bodie and Induſtrious Bent to Work therein; and BE IT KNOWNE that the Hon. Goodman Heinrich Verlac is thereby holding a General Call for Employe, and any Honeſt Man of Working Age is Encouraged to Applye his Self thereto. (an obituary, source unknown) Friends and Family will grieve the loss of Sgt. HOWARD BIEDERMEYER, who died on October 28, 1956 at the age of 46, of complications following a severe cerebral aneu- rysm. Howard was a loving husband and a devoted public servant. Services will be held this Satur- day at the Good Earth Funeral Home, 9:00 am. (a wedding announcement, source unknown) It is with great joy that Gwyneth and Paul Ward announce the engage- ment of their only daughter, JULIA GWENDOLYN WARD, to Dr. EDWARD PHILLIP VERLAC. Edward is an associate professor of history at Miskahawnee University in Anchor- head, Massachusetts; Julia plans to attend medical school at Inns- wich College. They plan a late June wedding. We wish them good fortune and long happiness in their union. The announcement is dated December 12, 1984, and is accompanied by a picture of the young couple. She has long, dark hair and a serious expression; he, although only in his twenties, is already starting to bald a little. You find yourself unable to tear your eyes away from his face for some time. It seems open and guileless, and he is clearly very happy. There is no hint there of the nightmare his world would one day become. Gallery A long room paneled in dark wood, with narrow windows at either end letting in little natural light. Paintings line one wall, mounted beneath small, shaded lamps that would illuminate the canvasses nicely if only the electricity were working. For what must be the hundredth time since you got up this morning, you wish you could open the windows in this place. Doorways lead north and east. There are four portraits hanging on the wall, each depicting one of the Verlacs' storied ancestors: Wilhelm, Heinrich, Elijah, and Mordecai. I only understood you as far as wanting to examine east. Back Hall A short hallway, connecting rooms to the east, west and north. Kitchen The kitchen has been picked completely clean; nothing but ranks of dusty cabinets and empty hooks line the walls. Doorways lead west and north, a small pantry lies to the east, and to the south, the back door leads out of the house. Dining Room A huge dining table runs the length of the room, its surface hidden under a thick blanket of dust. Above it, a crystal chandelier twinkles weakly in the gloom. Doorways lead elsewhere to the south and west. From here you can go south to the kitchen, or west to the foyer. Foyer A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north is the front door. Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Upstairs Hall The shuttered window at the end of the hall throws a gloomy rectangle of light onto the bare wooden floor. Doorways lead north and south. A cord dangles from the ceiling, right about level with your face. With a rusty, ratcheting groan and a brief shower of dust, the trap door swings down and unfolds into a rickety wooden ladder that leads up into darkness. Child's Bedroom This must once have been a child's bedroom — the bed is comically small compared to the one in the master bedroom. A little vanity table occupies the opposite wall, and in the corner under the window stands an antique crib. The only exit is to the south. On the vanity table is a jewelry box. You open the jewelry box, revealing a silver locket. In the jewelry box is a silver locket. The crib is made of wood and held together with pegs, not nails. It's probably at least as old as the house. (slipping the scrapbook into your pocket to make room) You take the silver locket from the jewelry box. The oval-shaped locket is held shut with a tiny clasp. The oval-shaped locket is held shut with a tiny clasp. You open the silver locket, revealing a picture of a boy inside. Upstairs Hall The shuttered window at the end of the hall throws a gloomy rectangle of light onto the bare wooden floor. Doorways lead north and south. A rickety ladder stands in the middle of the hall, descending from a three-foot by three-foot square of darkness in the ceiling. Library Dark paneling and a deep, green carpet give this room the feel of some blue-blooded country squire's literary refuge. Books line the walls from floor to ceiling, and a leather armchair sits near the window. To the west, a pair of sliding doors leads to the study, while the hall lies north. On the armchair is Ye Compleat Manuell of Wardes and Seales by Galarian Academius Deschamps. You can hear Michael in the next room, typing away. (slipping the silver locket into your pocket to make room) You pick up Ye Compleat Manuell of Wardes and Seales. An old medieval tome dating back to the 1300s. The cover is adorned with a gilded pentagram. You open Ye Compleat Manuell of Wardes and Seales and start reading. It seems to be a textbook on the theory and practice of magical wards and seals, mystic symbols inscribed on doors and containers in order to keep people and spirits out — or in. It's all very deliberately vague and arcane (to say nothing of having been originally written in medieval French and then translated into some variety of Middle English), and is probably the sort of thing that got many a scholar burned at the stake in those days. You are surprised to find that a page in this old and probably quite valuable book has been folded down, as though to mark the place. The section marked discusses various methods of opening doors that have been sealed with the image of a pagan god. According to this, speaking aloud the deity's true name will usually break the seal and open the door. You open the doors to see Michael at his desk, typing furiously. He looks up at you with a pained expression. "Hey, if you don't mind, I could really use the next hour or so to myself," he says. "I think I'm onto something really good here, and if I don't get it all out I'm going to lose it. Thanks, sweetie." You tiptoe out, shutting the doors quietly behind you. Upstairs Hall The shuttered window at the end of the hall throws a gloomy rectangle of light onto the bare wooden floor. Doorways lead north and south. A rickety ladder stands in the middle of the hall, descending from a three-foot by three-foot square of darkness in the ceiling. Child's Bedroom This must once have been a child's bedroom — the bed is comically small compared to the one in the master bedroom. A little vanity table occupies the opposite wall, and in the corner under the window stands an antique crib. The only exit is to the south. On the vanity table is a jewelry box. The flashlight emits a warm yellow beam. From here you can only go south to the upstairs hall. Upstairs Hall The shuttered window at the end of the hall throws a gloomy rectangle of light onto the bare wooden floor. Doorways lead north and south. A rickety ladder stands in the middle of the hall, descending from a three-foot by three-foot square of darkness in the ceiling. The rungs creak alarmingly as you ascend. Attic Dust motes hang motionless in the chill air, and grotesque, misshapen shadows crawl across the slanting rafters. To the east stands a wooden door, half-hidden in the gloom. A wooden ladder leads down to the second floor. (the attic door) It's an ordinary wooden door, though it looks sturdier than most. (the attic door) None of the keys you have on you seem to fit the lock. Upstairs Hall The shuttered window at the end of the hall throws a gloomy rectangle of light onto the bare wooden floor. Doorways lead north and south. A rickety ladder stands in the middle of the hall, descending from a three-foot by three-foot square of darkness in the ceiling. You switch the flashlight off. Child's Bedroom This must once have been a child's bedroom — the bed is comically small compared to the one in the master bedroom. A little vanity table occupies the opposite wall, and in the corner under the window stands an antique crib. The only exit is to the south. On the vanity table is a jewelry box. Upstairs Hall The shuttered window at the end of the hall throws a gloomy rectangle of light onto the bare wooden floor. Doorways lead north and south. A rickety ladder stands in the middle of the hall, descending from a three-foot by three-foot square of darkness in the ceiling. Library Dark paneling and a deep, green carpet give this room the feel of some blue-blooded country squire's literary refuge. Books line the walls from floor to ceiling, and a leather armchair sits near the window. To the west, a pair of sliding doors leads to the study, while the hall lies north. You can hear Michael in the next room, typing away. From here you can go north to the upstairs hall, or west. You can hear Michael in the next room, typing away. Upstairs Hall The shuttered window at the end of the hall throws a gloomy rectangle of light onto the bare wooden floor. Doorways lead north and south. A rickety ladder stands in the middle of the hall, descending from a three-foot by three-foot square of darkness in the ceiling. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Master Bedroom Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east. You can see a typewritten notice here. Bathroom A small room decorated in cold, white tile, with most of the space taken up by a large, cast iron bathtub. The bedroom lies west. A bath towel lies crumpled in the corner, next to the tub. Master Bedroom Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east. You can see a typewritten notice here. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Foyer A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north is the front door. Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer. You unlock the front door with the key to the house. You open the front door. Front of the House Gnarled and ancient trees surround a small clearing at the top of the hill. The road enters the clearing from the northwest and sweeps around in a wide curve, ending in front of the house. At the south end of the clearing stands the Verlac family mansion, its front door wide open. Scenic View Halfway up the hill, a break in the trees reveals a panoramic view of the Miskahawnee River Valley and the squalid little town of Anchorhead nestled within it. The road doubles back on itself, east to southeast, winding its tortuous way up to the top. A flurry of dead leaves skitters along the ground past you to the east. Chilly Avenue It is noticeably cooler along this lonely street at the edge of town, perhaps due to some unwholesome vapour rising from the waters of the nearby Miskahawnee. Dirt roads lead west and south, into the dense woods at the edge of town. Riverwalk This street follows along the mud-slick banks of the Miskahawnee before bending south, turning into a misty avenue between the trees. Steep, stone steps lead down to the water's edge. The town square lies west, and a vacant lot lies east. Town Square The claustrophobic streets open out here, onto this wide expanse of uneven pavestones in the center of town. The municipal courthouse stands at the south end of the square. Avenues to the west and east lead back into the cramped, ingrown streets, while to the north lies Whateley Bridge. In the center of the square, rising from a circular lawn of unhealthy-looking grass, stands a stone obelisk. A damp newspaper lies on the curb, fluttering slightly in the wind. (the Innswich Herald) (slipping Ye Compleat Manuell of Wardes and Seales into your pocket to make room) You pick up the newspaper. It's the Weekly Innswich Herald. Anchorhead, apparently, is not large enough to warrant its own newspaper. (the Innswich Herald) The front page story is about Jeffrey Greer, 8 years old, who was abducted from his home at #38 Mill Town Road last night. Little Jeffrey is the latest victim in a series of abductions that stretches back for years, and that authorities believe is the work of a single perpetrator. Early investigations led some to suspect Edward Verlac, Anchorhead resident and professor of history at Miskahawnee University, but local police were unable to obtain a confession or any hard proof. Verlac had been convicted of murdering his wife and two daughters in January of this year; he was found not guilty by reason of insanity and incarcerated in Dalmers Asylum, where he remained until committing suicide last March. This latest kidnapping, occurring months after Edward's death, seems to have cleared up any lingering suspicions that he might have been the culprit. Anyone possessing information regarding the whereabouts of Jeffrey Greer is strongly urged to speak to the authorities as soon as possible. There's also a weather advisory down in the lower corner of the page: heavy offshore fog today and tomorrow. Boaters and fishermen are urged to stay on shore; if you must go out on the water, navigate by the buoys and make sure to set a beacon before casting off. You climb the short flight of steps and enter the courthouse. Courthouse A long, dimly lit corridor stretches away from the front entrance. Closed, unmarked doors line either side of the hall, their pebbled glass windows lit from within by a murky, yellowish glow. Silence reigns here, except for your own footsteps echoing eerily on the tiled floor. Stairs at the south end of the hall lead down to the records department, or you can return to the town square to the north. Records Department A single bulb dangling from the ceiling casts its dirty dishwater light over a wasteland of papers, thousands of records and documents stacked everywhere in tottering piles. Stairs lead back up to ground level. There must be dozens of Verlacs listed in the archives. You'll have to be more specific than that. Fortunately you are able to find, buried under a massive pile of fishing license extension applications, a general index that lists records by name. After lots of sifting through shelves that haven't been organized since the Taft administration, you manage to pull up the records for Edward Verlac. Born July 17, 1959; died March 14, 1997. The death certificate lists the cause of death as "suicide." Courthouse A long, dimly lit corridor stretches away from the front entrance. Closed, unmarked doors line either side of the hall, their pebbled glass windows lit from within by a murky, yellowish glow. Silence reigns here, except for your own footsteps echoing eerily on the tiled floor. Stairs at the south end of the hall lead down to the records department, or you can return to the town square to the north. Town Square The claustrophobic streets open out here, onto this wide expanse of uneven pavestones in the center of town. The municipal courthouse stands at the south end of the square. Avenues to the west and east lead back into the cramped, ingrown streets, while to the north lies Whateley Bridge. In the center of the square, rising from a circular lawn of unhealthy-looking grass, stands a stone obelisk. The cold wind cuts through your clothes, chilling you to the bone. Whateley Bridge A hoary monument of crumbling, moss-eaten flagstones, Whateley Bridge is possibly older than any other structure in the entire town. It spans the dark Miskahawnee River, connecting the north and south halves of the city and occasionally raining bits of mortar from its underside into the torpid water. The bridge leads only north and south. The wind moans around eaves and over rooftops, like someone keening over a grave. The bridge leads only north and south. North Square To the north, a gap in the crowded press of buildings opens onto a road heading out over a grassy heath. The main street continues to the south towards Whateley Bridge and the center of town, while a narrow side street leads east. At the top of a steep drive to the northwest, you can just make out the ivy-covered walls of the university. Streets lead east, south, and northwest, or you can leave town to the north. University Courtyard The university campus huddles around this tiny, cobbled court, isolated and serene behind ivy-covered walls. City streets lie southeast. To the west is the library. Reading Room Most of the front area is taken up by reading desks, their green-shaded lamps casting tiny islands of radiance here and there amidst the library's dim interior. Shadows roost beneath the high, vaulted ceiling, and whispers rustle at the edge of your hearing. An exit lies east, and a small alcove to the north houses the circulation desk. Circulation Desk A high counter divides the reading area from the stacks. A small sign affixed to the wall next to the counter provides instructions for those seeking to check out books. On the counter are a service bell and a dog-eared register. Ding. A moment later, a gaunt and fishy-eyed librarian silently emerges from the shadows behind the counter. The librarian gives you a fishy look. It reads, "Please be prepared to show your faculty ID to the librarian. Books may not be removed from the library." It's a log, recording what book was checked out, who checked it out, and when. There are hundreds of entries in this old thing, too many to simply read through. You'd do better to look up a specific name. Flipping back through the yellowed pages, you notice Edward Verlac's name entered in the register several times throughout the year before his death. The books he checked out include A Historical Overview of Superstitions by Arnsworth Frazer, The Righteous Invasion by Warner Greene, Mechanics of Metempsychosis by C. C. H. Horne, N-Fold Transduction and the Space-Time Barrier by Lord Wheldrake, and Stranger Stars by Brian Monmouth. The librarian's mouth tightens in annoyance. Slowly, she extends one long, pale finger to point at the sign next to the counter. Again, the librarian points to the sign next to the counter. You can't see any such thing. The librarian purses her wide, strangely rubbery lips. She doesn't blink. Reading Room Most of the front area is taken up by reading desks, their green-shaded lamps casting tiny islands of radiance here and there amidst the library's dim interior. Shadows roost beneath the high, vaulted ceiling, and whispers rustle at the edge of your hearing. An exit lies east, and a small alcove to the north houses the circulation desk. University Courtyard The university campus huddles around this tiny, cobbled court, isolated and serene behind ivy-covered walls. City streets lie southeast. To the west is the library. North Square To the north, a gap in the crowded press of buildings opens onto a road heading out over a grassy heath. The main street continues to the south towards Whateley Bridge and the center of town, while a narrow side street leads east. At the top of a steep drive to the northwest, you can just make out the ivy-covered walls of the university. Mill Road The road leads you across a field of gray, windswept grass. To the south, the jagged outline of Anchorhead's roofs cuts across the horizon. The road forks here to the west and east, heading towards the old mill in one direction, out to the seashore in the other. The road heads west to the mill, east to the sea, or south back to town. In every other direction there is only empty heath. Mill Entrance The road from the east ends here in front of the grim fortress of charred brick that is the town's paper mill. A gate of heavy iron blocks the main entrance. To the southwest, a path leads to an overgrown area behind the mill, while to the north, a rutted lane leads to a small cluster of houses. A twelve-foot-high barricade of narrowly spaced iron bars seals off the entrance to the mill compound. It is shut and locked. There is no guardhouse, no buzzer or intercom, no handle or keyhole, no apparent means of getting in or letting anyone inside know you want in; just this implacable gate in an unscalable wall. The mill gate is locked tight. Several flakes of ash float past your face. Shanty Town The road from the south peters out at a small clearing of rutted mud, crowded close on all sides by leaning, dilapidated shacks. The town is still, almost deserted. Only a few loose bits of tarpaper, flapping listlessly in the wind, betray any movement. There is a soft rattle behind you, like an empty can being kicked across the uneven ground. But when you look, there is no one there. The sagging hovels have been leached gray by rain and mud and the pollution in the air. Here and there you spot a bit of color, some small attempt to humanize the place: a bright curtain behind a cracked window pane, or wilted flowers planted next to a warped and rotting front stoop . . . but by and large this is a joyless place, decrepit and groveling under the shadow of the mill. There are dozens of houses here. You might try referring to a specific address. After a moment or two of silence, you hear a furtive rattling, and the door opens a crack to reveal a woman's pale and haggard face. She stares out at you with a mixture of trepidation and mistrust. The woman's only answer is tight-lipped silence. She continues to stare at you, as though waiting to see what else you have to say. You spot a pair of lean, mangy dogs worrying at some refuse by one of the shacks. As soon as they notice you, they slink away. (first taking the silver locket from your pocket) She doesn't even look at it. Absently, you brush a bit of ash from your clothes; it leaves a grimy smear. As you turn to leave, the woman retreats into her home and closes the door. Mill Entrance The road from the east ends here in front of the grim fortress of charred brick that is the town's paper mill. A gate of heavy iron blocks the main entrance. To the southwest, a path leads to an overgrown area behind the mill, while to the north, a rutted lane leads to a small cluster of houses. A bit of ash blows into your face, stinging your eyes. Mill Road The road leads you across a field of gray, windswept grass. To the south, the jagged outline of Anchorhead's roofs cuts across the horizon. The road forks here to the west and east, heading towards the old mill in one direction, out to the seashore in the other. The wind kicks up a small cloud of ash, momentarily choking you. The road reaches the shore and heads out onto a rocky breakwater. The Spine You are picking your way across the rough, rock-strewn spine of the breakwater, where the road has diminished to little more than a pair of muddy ruts. A hundred yards to the northeast, at the breakwater's far end, a lonely lighthouse stands vigil against the ocean. Foot of the Lighthouse You stand in a circular clearing among the stones, surrounded on nearly every side by the sea. Before you looms the lighthouse, a massive pillar of ancient, pale bricks, jutting defiantly at the sky. The road leads southwest from here, and it looks as though a path leads around the outside of the lighthouse to the east. Set into the thick, stone wall of the lighthouse is a bronze door. None of the keys you have on you seem to fit the lock. The Spine You are picking your way across the rough, rock-strewn spine of the breakwater, where the road has diminished to little more than a pair of muddy ruts. A hundred yards to the northeast, at the breakwater's far end, a lonely lighthouse stands vigil against the ocean. The cold wind blows harder, tugging at the hem of your trenchcoat. You hear the lonely clanging of a buoy, somewhere far out to sea. Mill Road The road leads you across a field of gray, windswept grass. To the south, the jagged outline of Anchorhead's roofs cuts across the horizon. The road forks here to the west and east, heading towards the old mill in one direction, out to the seashore in the other. North Square To the north, a gap in the crowded press of buildings opens onto a road heading out over a grassy heath. The main street continues to the south towards Whateley Bridge and the center of town, while a narrow side street leads east. At the top of a steep drive to the northwest, you can just make out the ivy-covered walls of the university. Narrow Street As the street winds along from east to west, it narrows until the rooftops on either side practically touch each other. To the south is a twisting lane even narrower than this one, while a short flight of steps to the north leads down to the local watering hole. Twisting Lane The lane narrows here to little more than a badly cobbled sidewalk as it winds its way into one of the older neighborhoods. Buildings press close on every side, and tiny alleyways branch off in every direction. Someone has sprayed graffiti across a nearby wall. Hidden Courtyard This little courtyard is cleverly tucked away amidst a confusing tangle of darker avenues. Though overshadowed on all sides by the steep, forbidding rooftops, this secluded circle somehow manages to convey a sense of peace and brightness, a safe harbor from the oppressive gloom pervading the rest of the town. To the south, a hanging sign marks the entrance to a small shop, while a side street to the north leads back into the labyrinth. The cold wind cuts through your clothes, chilling you to the bone. A silvery bell jingles as you open the door. Curiosity Shop A warm and pleasantly dim light suffuses this cozy little shop. The shelves are crammed with odd knick-knacks and peculiar merchandise. In the rear of the store, a lit display case contains several items of interest. The courtyard lies outside, to the north. On the display case is a violin. The proprietor watches you quietly from behind the display case. The proprietor takes a handkerchief from his back pocket and polishes the top of the display case with it. It's an ordinary glass-fronted display case. Lined up inside are a velvet cloth, a tarot deck, a geode, and an amulet. Sitting on top is a violin. It's a beautiful instrument: its finish a rich chestnut color; its fingerboard gleaming ebony. The strings almost vibrate of their own accord, as though aching to be played. "Me? Oh, I'm just a harmless old shopkeeper, a lesser charlatan. I've had this place for . . . quite a while." The proprietor smiles at you in a way that is not entirely comforting. "I sit in my shop, sell my little goods. Keep an eye on things, generally." It's an unassuming silver pendant dangling from a thin leather cord, fashioned in the shape of a tiny, upturned hand with a triangle inscribed within the palm. It doesn't look like the fancy, New Age charms on the other shelves. It reminds you a little of one of those cast pewter game pieces from Monopoly. "Well now, there's a fancy little thing," remarks the proprietor. "A simple charm against evil. A ward against the Eye, you might say. Young chap brought that in — ten, fifteen years ago, I suppose. Said he had no more use for superstitious baubles." The proprietor reaches in through the back of the case and takes out the amulet. It gleams softly as he holds it up to the light. "I think, perhaps, that he eventually found different. And I think, perhaps, that you will, too." He leans forward and, before you can react, slips the leather cord around your neck. "But you won't make the same mistake, will you, Miss? No. You go on and keep that, now. My treat. You may find it useful soon." You already have the amulet. A hemisphere of rough, gray stone, about the size and shape of a halved cantaloupe. Its center is a glittering forest of violet crystals, thousands of sparkling, faceted stalactites and stalagmites inside a cave of colored glass. "Nice, isn't it?" beams the proprietor. "Chanced upon that while rock hunting up in the Cascades last summer." It's a beautiful set of cards laid out on a velvet cloth. The top card, "The Tower", has been turned up, allowing you to see the exquisitely detailed artwork. In this representation, the tower has been painted to resemble a lighthouse, broken by a bolt of lightning emanating from a great, red-rimmed eye. Two figures, a man and a woman, tumble from the tower's summit, cast lifelessly into the sea. "Mmm, yes, a rare piece of work, that. The cards are actually very delicate, thinly sliced ivory. Almost transparent. The box is walnut." He leans forward, offering you a salesman's conspiratorial wink. "They say," he whispers, "that this very deck was used and endorsed by the infamous medium. Er . . ." He frowns. " . . . not Blavatski; the other one. What was her name? Anyway, it is said that by shining a light through the cards and projecting the image onto a wall, you may open gateways to other worlds. Not that you'd want to, necessarily," he adds, frowning down at the upturned card. You have no purse and no money. "Be safe," says the proprietor. Hidden Courtyard This little courtyard is cleverly tucked away amidst a confusing tangle of darker avenues. Though overshadowed on all sides by the steep, forbidding rooftops, this secluded circle somehow manages to convey a sense of peace and brightness, a safe harbor from the oppressive gloom pervading the rest of the town. To the south, a hanging sign marks the entrance to a small shop, while a side street to the north leads back into the labyrinth. Twisting Lane The lane narrows here to little more than a badly cobbled sidewalk as it winds its way into one of the older neighborhoods. Buildings press close on every side, and tiny alleyways branch off in every direction. Someone has sprayed graffiti across a nearby wall. A gust of wind blows your hair into your face. Hidden Courtyard This little courtyard is cleverly tucked away amidst a confusing tangle of darker avenues. Though overshadowed on all sides by the steep, forbidding rooftops, this secluded circle somehow manages to convey a sense of peace and brightness, a safe harbor from the oppressive gloom pervading the rest of the town. To the south, a hanging sign marks the entrance to a small shop, while a side street to the north leads back into the labyrinth. You can leave the courtyard to the north, or enter the little shop to the south. You can leave the courtyard to the north, or enter the little shop to the south. You can leave the courtyard to the north, or enter the little shop to the south. Twisting Lane The lane narrows here to little more than a badly cobbled sidewalk as it winds its way into one of the older neighborhoods. Buildings press close on every side, and tiny alleyways branch off in every direction. Someone has sprayed graffiti across a nearby wall. The cold wind blows harder, tugging at the hem of your trenchcoat. You make your way through the dark, tangled streets, crossing your own path at least twice and stumbling through a series of filthy alleys that reek of rotting fish. It is not long before you are utterly lost. You take several blind turns, hoping to find some landmark you recognize, and suddenly find yourself emerging onto a . . . Vacant Lot Once a building stood here; now there is only cracked pavement and rampant weeds. Breaks in the chain-link fence lie east and west. An old man dressed in dirty rags sits on his mattress, muttering to himself as he stares intently out to sea. The chain-link fence surrounds the lot on all sides except for west and east. The chain-link fence surrounds the lot on all sides except for west and east. The old man clutches at something hanging around his neck. The cold wind cuts through your clothes, chilling you to the bone. Riverwalk This street follows along the mud-slick banks of the Miskahawnee before bending south, turning into a misty avenue between the trees. Steep, stone steps lead down to the water's edge. The town square lies west, and a vacant lot lies east. Streets lead east, west, and south from here, or you can venture down the steps. Vacant Lot Once a building stood here; now there is only cracked pavement and rampant weeds. Breaks in the chain-link fence lie east and west. An old man dressed in dirty rags sits on his mattress, muttering to himself as he stares intently out to sea. Once this pitiful creature might have been a man of health and dignity, but alcohol and mental illness have dragged him down irretrievably into degenerate squalor. His clothes are filthy tatters; his hair is a matted, knotted mess; the stench of booze and urine surrounds him. He sits on his mattress, seemingly oblivious to you or the rest of the world, and that's where he'll stay until either death or the next drink comes around. The old man rocks back and forth. The old man leans towards you. "Sorry, memory's not what it used to be . . ." he quavers. "So thirsty, you see, so very thirsty . . ." The old man hugs his knees and rocks back and forth, weeping softly. Riverwalk This street follows along the mud-slick banks of the Miskahawnee before bending south, turning into a misty avenue between the trees. Steep, stone steps lead down to the water's edge. The town square lies west, and a vacant lot lies east. Town Square The claustrophobic streets open out here, onto this wide expanse of uneven pavestones in the center of town. The municipal courthouse stands at the south end of the square. Avenues to the west and east lead back into the cramped, ingrown streets, while to the north lies Whateley Bridge. In the center of the square, rising from a circular lawn of unhealthy-looking grass, stands a stone obelisk. Shadowy Corner The buildings on this side of town are taller, and thus block more of the sky, making this neighborhood a particularly dark and unpleasant one. The main street leads away to the east, and a shadowy driveway leads through a high brick wall to the southwest. Asylum Courtyard This tiny, shadowed court is dominated by the grim edifice of Dalmers Asylum to the west. A driveway to the northeast leads back to the street, and a narrow alley leads to the east. Hidden Alley A featureless alley hidden deep in the shadows behind the courthouse. The opening to the northeast affords an excellent view of the whole town square and the obelisk at its center. Hidden Alley A featureless alley hidden deep in the shadows behind the courthouse. The opening to the northeast affords an excellent view of the whole town square and the obelisk at its center. Town Square The claustrophobic streets open out here, onto this wide expanse of uneven pavestones in the center of town. The municipal courthouse stands at the south end of the square. Avenues to the west and east lead back into the cramped, ingrown streets, while to the north lies Whateley Bridge. In the center of the square, rising from a circular lawn of unhealthy-looking grass, stands a stone obelisk. The obelisk measures about three feet square at its base, narrowing slightly as it rises a good fifteen feet to a blunt tip. It seems to be a monument of some sort, although you can see no plaque or marker anywhere near it. Dense, twisting hieroglyphs cover all four sides. Embedded in the stone high up on one side, very near the top, is an iron ring. The cold wind blows harder, tugging at the hem of your trenchcoat. Riverwalk This street follows along the mud-slick banks of the Miskahawnee before bending south, turning into a misty avenue between the trees. Steep, stone steps lead down to the water's edge. The town square lies west, and a vacant lot lies east. Chilly Avenue It is noticeably cooler along this lonely street at the edge of town, perhaps due to some unwholesome vapour rising from the waters of the nearby Miskahawnee. Dirt roads lead west and south, into the dense woods at the edge of town. Forest Lane The road winds gently through the birch trees south of town, continuing south into the forest. A footpath leads away to the east. Down the Road The forest grows denser as you go, crowding up to either side of the road. The groping, tangled branches block out what little sunlight there is left. Beyond the trees, the shadows are impenetrably thick. You walk a bit farther down the road, but it doesn't seem to lead anywhere, and the shadows grow darker the farther down you go. After a while you turn back, unable to suppress a shiver. Forest Lane The road winds gently through the birch trees south of town, continuing south into the forest. A footpath leads away to the east. Churchyard An old, abandoned church sits here in an overgrown clearing, surrounded by a sagging wrought-iron fence. A gap in the fence leads back west, while a bare path beaten through the weeds leads around the church to the southeast. The old church is built of massive, ancient stones, now black with age and nearly buried beneath an invading tide of ivy. The doors and windows are boarded shut. The broken steeple is a jagged spike against the sky. The path curves to the northeast as it follows the side of the church. Behind the Church Back here the forest grows right up to the fence, parts of which are literally buried in the tangled underbrush. All of the windows on this side of the church are boarded up as well. A path leads southwest, around to the front of the church. A wooden trap door over by the corner of the building probably leads into a cellar. The deranged cry of a whippoorwill echoes through the trees. The trap door is held shut with a rusty padlock. The trap door is held shut with a rusty padlock. The padlock is the size of your fist, and so thoroughly rusted that it's now little more than a homogeneous lump of oxidized metal. You might be able to break it if you had something heavy enough. You couldn't possibly break it with your bare hands. The broom is not heavy enough to do much damage to the padlock. A gust of wind blows your hair into your face. You find nothing of interest. Weeds and ground vines wind up through the bars, most of which are bent, broken, or missing altogether. The path curves to the northwest as it follows the side of the church. Churchyard An old, abandoned church sits here in an overgrown clearing, surrounded by a sagging wrought-iron fence. A gap in the fence leads back west, while a bare path beaten through the weeds leads around the church to the southeast. A flurry of dead leaves tumbles past you in the wind. Churchyard An old, abandoned church sits here in an overgrown clearing, surrounded by a sagging wrought-iron fence. A gap in the fence leads back west, while a bare path beaten through the weeds leads around the church to the southeast. You can't see any such thing. Forest Lane The road winds gently through the birch trees south of town, continuing south into the forest. A footpath leads away to the east. Chilly Avenue It is noticeably cooler along this lonely street at the edge of town, perhaps due to some unwholesome vapour rising from the waters of the nearby Miskahawnee. Dirt roads lead west and south, into the dense woods at the edge of town. The road climbs sharply, heading up into the hills. Scenic View Halfway up the hill, a break in the trees reveals a panoramic view of the Miskahawnee River Valley and the squalid little town of Anchorhead nestled within it. The road doubles back on itself, east to southeast, winding its tortuous way up to the top. In the distance, you can hear the lonesome keening of a train whistle drifting on the wind. Front of the House Gnarled and ancient trees surround a small clearing at the top of the hill. The road enters the clearing from the northwest and sweeps around in a wide curve, ending in front of the house. At the south end of the clearing stands the Verlac family mansion, its front door wide open. Foyer A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north the front door stands wide open. Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer. Doorways lead east, west, and south. Stairs lead up to the second floor, and you can leave the house to the north. Dining Room A huge dining table runs the length of the room, its surface hidden under a thick blanket of dust. Above it, a crystal chandelier twinkles weakly in the gloom. Doorways lead elsewhere to the south and west. Kitchen The kitchen has been picked completely clean; nothing but ranks of dusty cabinets and empty hooks line the walls. Doorways lead west and north, a small pantry lies to the east, and to the south, the back door leads out of the house. You unlock the back door with the key to the house. You open the back door. Overgrown Path A gravel path, nearly overgrown with weeds and briars, starts at the back door of the house and disappears into the undergrowth to the southeast, where the crooked trees seem to form a stunted, jagged archway into the dim recesses of the forest. The back door stands open. Stooping to avoid the sharp, bare twigs that snag your clothes and seem to reach greedily for your eyes, you pick your way through the undergrowth to a small clearing. Family Plot Moss-slimed tombstones, many of them broken off and leaning crazily like an old man's teeth, poke up from the soft, mulchy earth. The trees press close around this quiet enclave of death, leaning together over your head as if sharing secrets. A gap in the undergrowth provides an exit to the northwest. In the middle of the clearing stands a crumbling mausoleum, its heavy iron door nearly obscured by thick draperies of ivy. A grim pile of black granite, worn and weathered by centuries of neglect and unwholesome miasma. Above the iron door is engraved a single word: ~VERLAC~ You can't see any such thing. (first taking the old-fashioned iron key from your pocket) You unlock the iron door with the old-fashioned iron key. You open the iron door. The cold wind cuts through your clothes, chilling you to the bone. The flashlight emits a warm yellow beam. The musty smell of decay grows stronger as you descend. In the Crypt The air is frigid down here. The walls are damp and streaked with mud and lichen and worse things, and pale, swollen roots push through cracks in the masonry. The smell of corruption is almost overpowering. Stairs to the north lead back up to the surface. The walls of this chamber contain dozens of wide, shallow niches; in each niche, a coffin. One in particular catches your eye: a child's coffin, tiny and built from cheap pine. Unlike the others, the child's coffin is a ramshackle box of cheap pine, held together with rusting nails. It's half-rotten with age and so shabbily built you could probably open it with your bare hands. Compelled by a ghoulish curiosity, you dig your fingers under the soft, rotting boards and pry up the lid. With the shriek of pulling nails and a shower of crumbling wood, the coffin comes open, and you look inside, expecting to find a child's tiny skeleton . . . Dear God. There's no child buried here. Instead, the coffin contains only the worm-eaten bones of some kind of animal. Who would have buried an animal in place of the child that should have been interred here? Why would they do such a thing? You look around at the rest of the coffins, and glumly realize that anyone who might have known the answers to these questions is probably already buried in this room. Rank upon rank of the Verlac family remains are stacked away in this hole, left to crumble and deliquesce together, merging back into the dark matter which gave them birth. Above each niche is a nameplate, presumably indicating who was buried here and when they died. The nameplates are corroded with centuries of rot and verdigris and completely illegible. (the child's coffin) That's already open. The skeleton is in pieces; parts of it crumble even as you touch it. Only the animal's skull seems reasonably intact. (slipping the old-fashioned iron key into your pocket to make room) You pick up the dog's skull. The gruesome thing seems to leer at you with its bleached, toothy grin. Gratefully, you leave the noisome clamminess of the crypt behind. Family Plot Moss-slimed tombstones, many of them broken off and leaning crazily like an old man's teeth, poke up from the soft, mulchy earth. The trees press close around this quiet enclave of death, leaning together over your head as if sharing secrets. A gap in the undergrowth provides an exit to the northwest. The iron door of the mausoleum stands open, revealing steps leading down into subterranean gloom. You switch the flashlight off. You close the iron door. (first taking the old-fashioned iron key from your pocket) You lock the iron door. The wind moans fretfully. Overgrown Path A gravel path, nearly overgrown with weeds and briars, starts at the back door of the house and disappears into the undergrowth to the southeast, where the crooked trees seem to form a stunted, jagged archway into the dim recesses of the forest. The back door stands open. A flurry of dead leaves skitters along the ground past you in the wind. Kitchen The kitchen has been picked completely clean; nothing but ranks of dusty cabinets and empty hooks line the walls. Doorways lead west and north, a small pantry lies to the east, and to the south, the back door leads out of the house. The back door stands open. You close the back door. You lock the back door. Dining Room A huge dining table runs the length of the room, its surface hidden under a thick blanket of dust. Above it, a crystal chandelier twinkles weakly in the gloom. Doorways lead elsewhere to the south and west. Foyer A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north the front door stands wide open. Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer. You close the front door. You lock the front door. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Master Bedroom Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east. You can see a typewritten notice here. You're not particularly tired. There is no score in this story. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Foyer A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north is the front door. Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer. You unlock the front door with the key to the house. You open the front door. Front of the House Gnarled and ancient trees surround a small clearing at the top of the hill. The road enters the clearing from the northwest and sweeps around in a wide curve, ending in front of the house. At the south end of the clearing stands the Verlac family mansion, its front door wide open. Foyer A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north the front door stands wide open. Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer. Sitting Room A sparse but functional parlor. An old, colonial rug covers the floor, and the sofa is arranged so that visitors can enjoy the warmth of the large, ornate fireplace that fills the southern wall. Rooms lie to the east and south. The portrait over the fireplace glares down at you with relentless disapproval. Foyer A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north the front door stands wide open. Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer. You close the front door. You lock the front door. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Upstairs Hall The shuttered window at the end of the hall throws a gloomy rectangle of light onto the bare wooden floor. Doorways lead north and south. A rickety ladder stands in the middle of the hall, descending from a three-foot by three-foot square of darkness in the ceiling. Library Dark paneling and a deep, green carpet give this room the feel of some blue-blooded country squire's literary refuge. Books line the walls from floor to ceiling, and a leather armchair sits near the window. To the west, a pair of sliding doors leads to the study, while the hall lies north. You open the sliding doors. Study This room adjoining the library is small and cluttered, though in a comfortable, lived-in sort of way. A writing desk sits under the window, and a large, ornate fireplace fills the northern wall. Michael's laptop sits in the middle of the writing desk, humming quietly to itself. Sitting next to it is The Cryptical Haermoniacon. Although his computer is still on, Michael isn't here. In fact, you realize, you haven't heard him anywhere in the house since you got home. And you even found him a newspaper. It's a top-of-the-line model: sleek, lightweight, and very fast, a real status symbol for the aspiring writer who wants to look as though he might stop whatever he's doing at the drop of a hat and suddenly hack out a bestselling novel. While you have yet to witness Michael do this, it did make a nice gift to celebrate his new teaching position. The computer is in standby mode, blank except for a single window requesting a 4-digit user passcode. The full title is The Cryptical Haermoniacon: a Guide to the Atonal Servitors, and no author is listed anywhere on or in the book. Its leather covers are worn and cracked; the metal corner fittings tarnished; the pages yellow and brittle. You open The Cryptical Haermoniacon and start reading. The greater portion of the book is written in an incomprehensible, flowing cipher; however, the margins of several pages are glossed in English. The book appears to contain instructions for traveling to somewhere called "the Womb of Nehilim," and either worshipping or summoning (or possibly having sex with? — the text is very unclear) an entity that dwells there: The Watcher Who Is Named Not; nor may that Name be taught among His followers; that Name which may be learned only by gazing directly into His Awful Countenance . . . The specific details involve a ritual used to open a one-way portal into Nehilim, allowing the magician to enter or, more likely, throw some hapless soul into its chaotic and blasphemous depths. The ritual has something to do with music, and requires the magician to "attune the boundary pillars according to the vibrations of the resonant haermonicae." Whatever that means. The rest, by only the barest contrast, is nothing but pure raving. It's an ordinary writing desk Michael bought from Ikea. No drawers or secret compartments that you're aware of. On the writing desk are Michael's laptop and The Cryptical Haermoniacon. The mantelpiece is carved from dark, polished rosewood and covers the whole wall. It is identical to the downstairs fireplace in most respects, except for the pair of brass spheres mounted on either end. You're miles from any working phone. You see the room behind you in distorted miniature, your own face swelling and stretching comically across the sphere's reflective surface. Looking closely at one of the spheres, you can make out smudged fingerprints marring the otherwise immaculately polished brass. The sphere twists clockwise a few degrees. There is a barely audible click, and the sphere snaps back to its original position. The entire fireplace slides some eighteen inches to the left with a hollow grinding sound, revealing a narrow corridor leading northeast. The flashlight emits a warm yellow beam. Behind the Walls Bare wooden slats, patched with crumbling plaster and spiked with bent, rusted nails, press in close against your shoulders. Side passages branch off in strange directions that don't seem like they should fit within the floorplan of the house. Behind the Walls Bare wooden slats, patched with crumbling plaster and spiked with bent, rusted nails, press in close against your shoulders. Side passages branch off in strange directions that don't seem like they should fit within the floorplan of the house. The passage doesn't seem go in that direction. It doesn't seem to be following the shape of the walls as you remember them. You're not even entirely sure which floor you're on. You hear nothing unexpected. You can't see any such thing. That's not a verb I recognize. The passage doesn't seem go in that direction. The passage bends and twists at confusing angles, making it hard to tell which direction you're heading in. You stumble along as best you can. Behind the Walls Bare wooden slats, patched with crumbling plaster and spiked with bent, rusted nails, press in close against your shoulders. Side passages branch off in strange directions that don't seem like they should fit within the floorplan of the house. A narrow, wooden ladder leads up. Observatory Going by the view through the dingy skylight over your head, you'd guess that you are inside the cupola on the roof of the house. The room is tiny and cluttered, with torn and faded astronomical charts covering every wall. A ladder leads down through a hatch in the floor. In center of the room stands a large mounted telescope, pointed almost straight up through the skylight. The stars on these charts look exactly like those you saw in the mural, down in the wine cellar, though here at least they are clearly labeled. The constellations they form are disturbing and unfamiliar, with pictures of worms and toads and other, less pleasant things drawn along the line of the zodiac. The margins are filled with diagrams and annotations made in crabbed, stilted handwriting. The annotations seem to be calculating the trajectory of one comet in particular, as it gradually approaches the solar system over the course of several centuries. According to these equations, the comet is due to arrive . . . Well, that's interesting. According to this, the comet is coming tomorrow. The telescope is an unadorned cylinder made of greenish metal, about eight inches in diameter and nearly four feet long. A thin, rectangular slot is cut into one side, about six inches from the top. Near the bottom, an eyepiece juts out to one side. The sky is overcast; you can't see anything through the telescope beyond the blank, gray circle of solid clouds. Behind the Walls Bare wooden slats, patched with crumbling plaster and spiked with bent, rusted nails, press in close against your shoulders. Side passages branch off in strange directions that don't seem like they should fit within the floorplan of the house. A narrow, wooden ladder leads up. You stumble along as best you can. Behind the Walls Bare wooden slats, patched with crumbling plaster and spiked with bent, rusted nails, press in close against your shoulders. Side passages branch off in strange directions that don't seem like they should fit within the floorplan of the house. You notice a small hole about halfway up one wall. Standing on tiptoe and placing your eye up to the hole, you see the wine cellar, seen from low down on the south wall. The view is strangely grainy and washed-out at the edges, like a scene from an old movie. The passage doesn't seem go in that direction. The passage doesn't seem go in that direction. You stumble along as best you can. Behind the Walls Bare wooden slats, patched with crumbling plaster and spiked with bent, rusted nails, press in close against your shoulders. Side passages branch off in strange directions that don't seem like they should fit within the floorplan of the house. A narrow, wooden ladder leads up. You stumble along as best you can. Behind the Walls Bare wooden slats, patched with crumbling plaster and spiked with bent, rusted nails, press in close against your shoulders. Side passages branch off in strange directions that don't seem like they should fit within the floorplan of the house. The passage doesn't seem go in that direction. The passage doesn't seem go in that direction. The passage doesn't seem go in that direction. Behind the Walls Bare wooden slats, patched with crumbling plaster and spiked with bent, rusted nails, press in close against your shoulders. Side passages branch off in strange directions that don't seem like they should fit within the floorplan