-blooded country squire's literary refuge. Books line the walls from floor to ceiling, and a leather armchair sits near the window. To the west, a pair of sliding doors opens onto the study, while the hall lies north. Upstairs Hall The shuttered window at the end of the hall throws a gloomy rectangle of light onto the bare wooden floor. Doorways lead north and south. A rickety ladder stands in the middle of the hall, descending from a three-foot by three-foot square of darkness in the ceiling. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Master Bedroom Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east. You can see a typewritten notice here. From here you can go north to the upstairs landing, or east to the bathroom. You're not particularly tired. Time passes. Time passes. Time passes. Time passes. Time passes. What a good idea. I only understood you as far as wanting to think. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Master Bedroom Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east. You can see a typewritten notice here. You're not particularly tired. A flawless mirror in a beautifully scrolled walnut frame. You look good, considering you're covered in grimy ash and all scratched up from picking your way through the underbrush and thickets. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Foyer A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north is the front door. Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer. That's already closed. That's locked at the moment. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Master Bedroom Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east. You can see a typewritten notice here. You can't see any such thing. You can't see any such thing. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Master Bedroom Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east. You can see a typewritten notice here. Bathroom A small room decorated in cold, white tile, with most of the space taken up by a large, cast iron bathtub. The bedroom lies west. A bath towel lies crumpled in the corner, next to the tub. From here you can only go west to the master bedroom. From here you can only go west to the master bedroom. From here you can only go west to the master bedroom. From here you can only go west to the master bedroom. From here you can only go west to the master bedroom. Master Bedroom Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east. You can see a typewritten notice here. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Foyer A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north is the front door. Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer. You unlock the front door with the key to the house. You open the front door. Front of the House Gnarled and ancient trees surround a small clearing at the top of the hill. The road enters the clearing from the northwest and sweeps around in a wide curve, ending in front of the house. At the south end of the clearing stands the Verlac family mansion, its front door wide open. The cold wind cuts through your clothes, chilling you to the bone. Scenic View Halfway up the hill, a break in the trees reveals a panoramic view of the Miskahawnee River Valley and the squalid little town of Anchorhead nestled within it. The road doubles back on itself, east to southeast, winding its tortuous way up to the top. Chilly Avenue It is noticeably cooler along this lonely street at the edge of town, perhaps due to some unwholesome vapour rising from the waters of the nearby Miskahawnee. Dirt roads lead west and south, into the dense woods at the edge of town. Riverwalk This street follows along the mud-slick banks of the Miskahawnee before bending south, turning into a misty avenue between the trees. Steep, stone steps lead down to the water's edge. The town square lies west, and a vacant lot lies east. Town Square The claustrophobic streets open out here, onto this wide expanse of uneven pavestones in the center of town. The municipal courthouse stands at the south end of the square. Avenues to the west and east lead back into the cramped, ingrown streets, while to the north lies Whateley Bridge. In the center of the square, rising from a circular lawn of unhealthy-looking grass, stands a stone obelisk. Riverwalk This street follows along the mud-slick banks of the Miskahawnee before bending south, turning into a misty avenue between the trees. Steep, stone steps lead down to the water's edge. The town square lies west, and a vacant lot lies east. The cold wind blows harder, tugging at the hem of your trenchcoat. Vacant Lot Once a building stood here; now there is only cracked pavement and rampant weeds. Breaks in the chain-link fence lie east and west. An old man dressed in dirty rags sits on his mattress, muttering to himself as he stares intently out to sea. The old man shakes his head mutely. "Sorry . . . sorry . . ." he quavers. "I'm so very thirsty, so very thirsty . . ." (first taking the dog's skull from your pocket) The old man seems to have a hard time focusing on the dog's skull. "Sorry . . . sorry . . ." he quavers. "I'm so very thirsty, you see, so very thirsty . . ." The wind moans around eaves and over rooftops, as though it had forgotten something terribly important. Riverwalk This street follows along the mud-slick banks of the Miskahawnee before bending south, turning into a misty avenue between the trees. Steep, stone steps lead down to the water's edge. The town square lies west, and a vacant lot lies east. Town Square The claustrophobic streets open out here, onto this wide expanse of uneven pavestones in the center of town. The municipal courthouse stands at the south end of the square. Avenues to the west and east lead back into the cramped, ingrown streets, while to the north lies Whateley Bridge. In the center of the square, rising from a circular lawn of unhealthy-looking grass, stands a stone obelisk. Whateley Bridge A hoary monument of crumbling, moss-eaten flagstones, Whateley Bridge is possibly older than any other structure in the entire town. It spans the dark Miskahawnee River, connecting the north and south halves of the city and occasionally raining bits of mortar from its underside into the torpid water. North Square To the north, a gap in the crowded press of buildings opens onto a road heading out over a grassy heath. The main street continues to the south towards Whateley Bridge and the center of town, while a narrow side street leads east. At the top of a steep drive to the northwest, you can just make out the ivy-covered walls of the university. Narrow Street As the street winds along from east to west, it narrows until the rooftops on either side practically touch each other. To the south is a twisting lane even narrower than this one, while a short flight of steps to the north leads down to the local watering hole. Local Pub A dank and claustrophobic hole in the wall. Greasy smoke pools amidst the low-hanging rafters, and the smell of decades' worth of beer and probably worse oozes from the floorboards. An exit to the south leads back out to the street. An old lantern casts its dim, flickering glow across the bar. Someone has carelessly left a flask of whiskey behind. (slipping the dog's skull into your pocket to make room) You pick up the flask. The label is faded and mostly peeled away, but what little you can make out seems to indicate that this is not a particularly distinguished brand of spirits. The words "bootleg" and "rotgut" come to mind, as does the phrase "methanol-induced blindness". Narrow Street As the street winds along from east to west, it narrows until the rooftops on either side practically touch each other. To the south is a twisting lane even narrower than this one, while a short flight of steps to the north leads down to the local watering hole. Twisting Lane The lane narrows here to little more than a badly cobbled sidewalk as it winds its way into one of the older neighborhoods. Buildings press close on every side, and tiny alleyways branch off in every direction. Someone has sprayed graffiti across a nearby wall. You make your way through the dark, tangled streets, navigating a sharp turn that seems to curve inward on itself like a nautilus shell and squeezing through alleys so tight that your shoulders brush the damp, crumbling mortar on either side. You take several blind turns and suddenly find yourself back at the . . . Twisting Lane The lane narrows here to little more than a badly cobbled sidewalk as it winds its way into one of the older neighborhoods. Buildings press close on every side, and tiny alleyways branch off in every direction. Someone has sprayed graffiti across a nearby wall. Hidden Courtyard This little courtyard is cleverly tucked away amidst a confusing tangle of darker avenues. Though overshadowed on all sides by the steep, forbidding rooftops, this secluded circle somehow manages to convey a sense of peace and brightness, a safe harbor from the oppressive gloom pervading the rest of the town. To the south, a hanging sign marks the entrance to a small shop, while a side street to the north leads back into the labyrinth. A gust of wind blows your hair into your face. "Back again so soon?" asks the proprietor. Curiosity Shop A warm and pleasantly dim light suffuses this cozy little shop. The shelves are crammed with odd knick-knacks and peculiar merchandise. In the rear of the store, a lit display case contains several items of interest. The courtyard lies outside, to the north. On the display case is a violin. The proprietor watches you quietly from behind the display case. From here you can only go north. From here you can only go north. The proprietor waves as you leave. "Come back soon," he calls. Hidden Courtyard This little courtyard is cleverly tucked away amidst a confusing tangle of darker avenues. Though overshadowed on all sides by the steep, forbidding rooftops, this secluded circle somehow manages to convey a sense of peace and brightness, a safe harbor from the oppressive gloom pervading the rest of the town. To the south, a hanging sign marks the entrance to a small shop, while a side street to the north leads back into the labyrinth. Twisting Lane The lane narrows here to little more than a badly cobbled sidewalk as it winds its way into one of the older neighborhoods. Buildings press close on every side, and tiny alleyways branch off in every direction. Someone has sprayed graffiti across a nearby wall. You make your way through the dark, tangled streets, finding at least one relatively wide and well-lit avenue that is pleasant to stroll down and stumbling through a series of filthy alleys that reek of rotting fish. You take several blind turns and suddenly find yourself back at the . . . Shadowy Corner The buildings on this side of town are taller, and thus block more of the sky, making this neighborhood a particularly dark and unpleasant one. The main street leads away to the east, and a shadowy driveway leads through a high brick wall to the southwest. Streets lead east and southwest from here. Town Square The claustrophobic streets open out here, onto this wide expanse of uneven pavestones in the center of town. The municipal courthouse stands at the south end of the square. Avenues to the west and east lead back into the cramped, ingrown streets, while to the north lies Whateley Bridge. In the center of the square, rising from a circular lawn of unhealthy-looking grass, stands a stone obelisk. Riverwalk This street follows along the mud-slick banks of the Miskahawnee before bending south, turning into a misty avenue between the trees. Steep, stone steps lead down to the water's edge. The town square lies west, and a vacant lot lies east. In the distance, you can hear the lonesome keening of a train whistle drifting on the wind. Vacant Lot Once a building stood here; now there is only cracked pavement and rampant weeds. Breaks in the chain-link fence lie east and west. An old man dressed in dirty rags sits on his mattress, muttering to himself as he stares intently out to sea. His eyes burning with greed, the old man snatches the flask from your hand, unscrews the cap, and upturns it into his eager mouth. Trickles of pungent brown liquid ooze from the corners of his mouth as he swallows convulsively, his hands trembling, his eyes squeezed shut and streaming tears. Finally, he falls back onto his mattress with a beatific smile on his mud-smeared face, the flask dropping from his fingers. The old man squints at you suspiciously. "Eh? What are you goin' on about, coffins and burials and such?" Don't know what you mean, so I don't." The wind moans around eaves and over rooftops, as though beset by troubling dreams. (first taking the dog's skull from your pocket) The old man bats the skull away with an expression of disgust. "What're you stickin'that in my face for?" he demands. "Just whose do you think it is?" "Shhh!" hisses the old man, interrupting you. He peers furtively out at the shadows beyond the chain-link fence. "Hear that?" he asks, giving you a sly wink. "That's the maggots. Everywhere, listening. So they are." The old man sighs, and for a moment he looks and sounds relatively lucid. "Oh, yes, I was a doctor, once. I was a very good doctor. But that was before Anna, you know. Anna and little Edward and —" Suddenly he stops, and will say no more. The old man looks greedily at the amulet, but seems too nervous to take it. The old man looks wistful. "Oh, Anna, she was such a sweet girl, you know. She never deserved it, never deserved it. She came to me when . . ." He falters, and tears begin to stream unchecked down his face. "It shouldn't have happened to the girl. I tried, I was a good doctor, but only fifteen she was, still a child when he . . . when that red-eyed monster took her . . ." His eyes suddenly darken, his fists clench. "HIS OWN DAUGHTER! GOD DAMM THOSE MAGGOTS! SHOULDN'T HAVE DONE THAT TO HIS OWN DAUGHTER! GOD DAMN 'EM ALL!" The old man lets out a vicious cackle. "Oh, they got poor Edward, so they did! Maggots got 'im good. Got into his head, you know. The maggots got into his head, an' they et up the insides of it 'til he had to crack it open to get 'em out. Poor boy." Suddenly he becomes somber. "Poor boy. Delivered him, I did, you know. Pulled him right out of his mama, spanked his little behind. I know, I know. Little Edward and . . . and his . . ." His voice trails off, but you could swear he mouthed the word "brother". The old man seems to physically shrink at the sound of the Verlac name. "Oh, they're the worst of the lot," he whispers. "They're the queen maggots, so they are. I know. Sittin' all fat and full of poison in the center of the nest. Now, Edward . . . Edward warn't so bad, no, but his — his grandfather, he was one o' the worst, the worst of all, oh yes." For a while the old man simply stares down at his hands, limp and open in his lap. Then, drawing a deep breath, he begins to speak, and for the first time his voice is steady, with no trace of the alcoholic's craving whine. "Yes, yes, little Edward had a brother, so he did. And I know him. I know of William, so I do. I delivered the child. Anna Verlac was his mother. It . . . it was . . ." He leans over and grips your arm with a hand that is suddenly steady and strong. "It was her father's poison seed, y'see. That evil bastard poisoned her womb and put a monster inside her! What she gave birth to . . . it was . . . it warn't . . . oh, God, when I held that . . . that abortion in the shape of an infant, that thing with its hooves and its writhin' . . . all of its father's evil given flesh . . . except for its eyes. It had its mother's eyes, and its face was all the more dreadful for it." The old man shudders convulsively, and it is a while before he can speak again. "Anna and I decided between ourselves, we did. The . . . her child . . . it lived for a couple of years, but it was sickly, and in pain, and it could never be normal, nor healthy. She brought it to me . . . I was her doctor, you see, I'd seen her through her child's birth, and she trusted me . . . and I — I put the thing down. Softly, without pain. They laid it to rest in the family crypt. "And Anna thanked me for it, so she did." The old man falls silent, and his wavering, bloodshot eyes wander once more out to sea. And he is lost, once again, to the horror and pain of the memories that can only be silenced by drink. The cold wind cuts through your clothes, chilling you to the bone. The old man looks greedily at the amulet, but seems too nervous to take it. The old man hugs his knees and rocks back and forth, weeping softly. "All I care to tell of that tragedy, I've told," the old man says quietly, not looking at you. "William Verlac is dead and buried in the family crypt, and I've no wish to speak of it again." The old man grows sickly pale. He raises a hand as if to ward off the sight of the skull, to deny the truth one more time; then his hand drops, and he sinks down onto the mattress, groaning. "Oh, it's true," he cries, "it's true, God help my lying soul. It was her father who wished to kill the thing, not Anna — 'kill it to make way for another,' he said. 'A more fitting vessel.' He wanted to try again — God! The depth of his evil — after the way the first one came out, he wanted to try again! "Anna came to me and asked for my help, and what could I do? What could I do? It was her child, for God's sake, no matter what else it was, and she couldn't bear to lose it. We took it to the woods, south of town, and left it there, and we weighed the coffin down with the carcass of a dog, to fool her father." The old man stares at his hands in broken despair. "Then, her father . . . he did what he did again, and that was Edward. Anna was never the same after that, and she died only a few years later. And her William . . . William's still out there, and the blood of every child he's taken is on my hands, because I couldn't bring myself to put him down, all those years ago." He sits up suddenly, fastening on you with a sick, pleading look. "I've told you too much, now," he whispers desperately. "They'll know; the maggots'll find out for sure. They'll find me; even in my hole, they'll find me." He lets out an eye-wateringly pungent breath. "P'raps it's best they do. I've spent so long drownin' my sins, p'raps time's come to pay for them at last. This won't do me much good no more," he says, holding up a little copper key. "And yet . . ." He closes his fist around it again. "And yet, maybe there's a way. They say there's ways to protect yourself, wards versus the evil eye and such." The old man stares greedily at the amulet around your neck. "P'raps if I could get me one o' those . . ." "YOU DON'T KNOW! YOU DON'T KNOW!" the old man shrieks hoarsely. "I CAN GO WHERE YOU CAN'T GO!" "Thank you kindly, Miss, you're a generous one, so you are," grins the old man as he closes his grimy fingers around the charm. "And here y'are," he says, tossing you the copper key. "I'll pray it does you good." He staggers to his feet. "I'll be gettin' on, then. If I can make away before nightfall, p'raps I'll be out of their reach before the maggots come out." He wraps the leather thong around his hand, closing the charm in his fist, and turns to you one last time. "Don't let 'em find you," he says earnestly. "Get out while you can. If you stay, they'll track you down eventually. It's the blood, see. He can smell it, so he can. And he always returns to his blood." And then he shambles off, up the riverwalk towards the town square, his shadow dragging long behind him. Looking to the sky, you realize that the sun is nearly set. You've been talking to the old man for much longer than you thought, and it will be dark soon. Riverwalk This street follows along the mud-slick banks of the Miskahawnee before bending south, turning into a misty avenue between the trees. Steep, stone steps lead down to the water's edge. The town square lies west, and a vacant lot lies east. Town Square The claustrophobic streets open out here, onto this wide expanse of uneven pavestones in the center of town. The municipal courthouse stands at the south end of the square. Avenues to the west and east lead back into the cramped, ingrown streets, while to the north lies Whateley Bridge. In the center of the square, rising from a circular lawn of unhealthy-looking grass, stands a stone obelisk. You climb the short flight of steps and enter the courthouse. Courthouse A long, dimly lit corridor stretches away from the front entrance. Closed, unmarked doors line either side of the hall, their pebbled glass windows lit from within by a murky, yellowish glow. Silence reigns here, except for your own footsteps echoing eerily on the tiled floor. Stairs at the south end of the hall lead down to the records department, or you can return to the town square to the north. The office doors are all locked. The office doors are all locked. Records Department A single bulb dangling from the ceiling casts its dirty dishwater light over a wasteland of papers, thousands of records and documents stacked everywhere in tottering piles. Stairs lead back up to ground level. Courthouse A long, dimly lit corridor stretches away from the front entrance. Closed, unmarked doors line either side of the hall, their pebbled glass windows lit from within by a murky, yellowish glow. Silence reigns here, except for your own footsteps echoing eerily on the tiled floor. Stairs at the south end of the hall lead down to the records department, or you can return to the town square to the north. Town Square The claustrophobic streets open out here, onto this wide expanse of uneven pavestones in the center of town. The municipal courthouse stands at the south end of the square. Avenues to the west and east lead back into the cramped, ingrown streets, while to the north lies Whateley Bridge. In the center of the square, rising from a circular lawn of unhealthy-looking grass, stands a stone obelisk. A flurry of dead leaves whirls past you in the wind. Riverwalk This street follows along the mud-slick banks of the Miskahawnee before bending south, turning into a misty avenue between the trees. Steep, stone steps lead down to the water's edge. The town square lies west, and a vacant lot lies east. The cold wind blows harder, tugging at the hem of your trenchcoat. Chilly Avenue It is noticeably cooler along this lonely street at the edge of town, perhaps due to some unwholesome vapour rising from the waters of the nearby Miskahawnee. Dirt roads lead west and south, into the dense woods at the edge of town. The road climbs sharply, heading up into the hills. Scenic View Halfway up the hill, a break in the trees reveals a panoramic view of the Miskahawnee River Valley and the squalid little town of Anchorhead nestled within it. The road doubles back on itself, east to southeast, winding its tortuous way up to the top. Front of the House Gnarled and ancient trees surround a small clearing at the top of the hill. The road enters the clearing from the northwest and sweeps around in a wide curve, ending in front of the house. At the south end of the clearing stands the Verlac family mansion, its front door wide open. Foyer A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south into pitch-black rooms, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north the front door stands wide open. Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer. You call out to let Michael know you're home, but no one answers. The house is still, hushed. Prickles of unease crawl down your back like centipede legs. Michael isn't here, you realize. The house is empty. You are alone. You close the front door. You lock the front door. That's already on. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Master Bedroom Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east. You can see a typewritten notice here. You switch the flashlight off. You're covered in grimy ash and all scratched up from picking your way through the underbrush and thickets; maybe you should take a bath first. Bathroom A small room decorated in cold, white tile, with most of the space taken up by a large, cast iron bathtub. The bedroom lies west. A bath towel lies crumpled in the corner, next to the tub. The flashlight emits a warm yellow beam. Grateful for the chance to relax, you strip out of your clothes, draw a hot bath (the gas, at least, must be running, even if nothing else in the house is) and soak. The tension of this strange day melts gently from your body. After thirty minutes or so, you begin to feel normal again. After forty-five, you begin to think you might be able to adjust to this place after all. By then the water has begun to grow cool, however, so you drain it and quietly dry yourself off. All in all, you feel enormously better. (first taking your clothes) You put on your clothes. You switch the flashlight off. Master Bedroom Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east. You can see a typewritten notice here. You take off your clothes, crawl into bed and pull the covers up to your chin. You toss and turn for a few minutes, plagued by the nagging sensation that you've forgotten something. Suddenly you remember — you've left the front door locked, and you have the only key. If Michael comes back in the middle of the night, he won't be able to get in. Reluctantly, you do so. (first taking your clothes) You put on your clothes. You can't see any such thing. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Foyer A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south into pitch-black rooms, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north is the front door. Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer. I only understood you as far as wanting to unlock the front door with the house window. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Master Bedroom Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east. You can see a typewritten notice here. You can't see any such thing. You take off your clothes, crawl into bed and pull the covers up to your chin. You toss and turn for a few minutes, plagued by the nagging sensation that you've forgotten something. Suddenly you remember — you've left the front door locked, and you have the only key. If Michael comes back in the middle of the night, he won't be able to get in. Reluctantly, you do so. You are wearing your wedding ring. Master Bedroom Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east. Your clothes lie in an untidy pile on the floor. You can also see a typewritten notice here. (first taking your clothes) You put on your clothes. Bathroom A small room decorated in cold, white tile, with most of the space taken up by a large, cast iron bathtub. The bedroom lies west. A bath towel lies crumpled in the corner, next to the tub. You can also see your trenchcoat, a keyring with a couple of keys on it, your umbrella, a broom, a dog's skull, and a thick copper key here. Bathroom A small room decorated in cold, white tile, with most of the space taken up by a large, cast iron bathtub. The bedroom lies west. A bath towel lies crumpled in the corner, next to the tub. You can also see your trenchcoat, a keyring with a couple of keys on it, your umbrella, a broom, a dog's skull, and a thick copper key here. keyring: Taken. thick copper key: You pick up the thick copper key. The key is thick and stubby, like it's meant to unlock something heavy. Printed on one side are the words, Miskahawnee River Valley Public Works. trenchcoat: Taken. umbrella: Taken. broom: (slipping the umbrella into the pocket of your trenchcoat to make room) You pick up the broom. dog's skull: (slipping the thick copper key into the pocket of your trenchcoat to make room) Taken. bath towel: (slipping the dog's skull into the pocket of your trenchcoat to make room) You pick up the bath towel. You put on the trenchcoat. Master Bedroom Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east. You can see a typewritten notice here. Master Bedroom Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east. You can see a typewritten notice here. You are wearing your trenchcoat and your wedding ring, and holding a bath towel, a broom, and a keyring with a couple of keys on it. The pockets of your trenchcoat contain a dog's skull, a thick copper key, your umbrella, an old-fashioned iron key, a silver locket, a newspaper, Ye Compleat Manuell of Wardes and Seales, a scrapbook, and a flashlight. You take the flashlight from your pocket. The flashlight emits a warm yellow beam. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Foyer A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south into pitch-black rooms, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north is the front door. Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer. You unlock the front door with the key to the house. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Master Bedroom Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east. You can see a typewritten notice here. You switch the flashlight off. You take off your clothes, leave your things on the floor, crawl into bed and pull the covers up to your chin. You lie awake for what seems like hours, listening to the house creak and settle around you and wondering where your husband is. You try to convince yourself that he merely went for a walk and lost track of the time, as he is so often wont to do. In any case, with no telephone and no car, there is little you can do besides hope that he will turn up in the morning. With your worried thoughts chasing themselves endlessly around your mind, you eventually drift off into an uneasy sleep . . . You are running. Down the road, through the woods, where the whippoorwills call their lunatic's song, you are running. The sky boils and thunders overhead. Red-rimmed eyes glare down at you through the branches of the trees. When you look up at them, they merge together, melting into a single, baleful orb — a swollen, bloody moon. Vines and brambles tear at your clothes. Rocks cut your feet. Still you must run, until you suddenly burst out of the underbrush, into a tangled, moonlit clearing. An ancient, flagstone well stands amidst the weeds in the clearing's center. You are drawn to it. Something is moving inside, scratching at the stones down in that circle of blackness, and somewhere you hear a child's voice whispering: the cellar, Michael is in the cellar, and he is doing something bad . . . You lean over the well's crumbling lip. Your heart is pounding. Your hair slips from your shoulders, dangling tantalizingly close to the black opening . . . . . . the whippoorwills reach a crescendo . . . Your eyes adjust to the dark and you see — — you see the most horrible thing — THE THIRD DAY If heaven is merciful, it will some day efface from my consciousness the sight that I saw, and let me live my last years in peace. — H. P. Lovecraft, "The Lurking Fear" You wake up, shivering. Michael is in bed next to you, sleeping like a dead man with the sheets in a twisted snarl around his body. Master Bedroom (on the four-poster bed) Pale yellow wallpaper and sketches hanging from the walls lend this room a bit of faded, rustic charm. Almost half the room is dominated by an enormous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east. Your clothes lie in an untidy pile on the floor. Michael is sprawled across the bed, fast asleep. You can also see a keyring with a couple of keys on it, a broom, a bath towel, a flashlight, your trenchcoat, and a typewritten notice here. Carefully, so as not to wake Michael, you do so. (first taking your clothes) You put on your clothes. Taken. Taken. You pick up the broom. Michael comes awake with a jerk and a hoarse shout. For a moment or two he only stares about blindly, as if unsure of where he is and all but unaware of your presence. Then, with a grunt of surprise, he seems to come to his senses and painstakingly crawls out of bed. "I'm fine . . . I'm fine," grumbles Michael, although he clearly is not. "I'll feel better in a little while." Michael stumbles off to the upstairs landing. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Michael is here, frowning and absently rubbing at his forehead. Michael goes off to the upstairs hall, muttering something about "being watched . . ." You can't see any such thing. Upstairs Hall The shuttered window at the end of the hall throws a gloomy rectangle of light onto the bare wooden floor. Doorways lead north and south. Michael is here. He flashes you a guilty look as you enter the room, as though you'd caught him at something. A rickety ladder stands in the middle of the hall, descending from a three-foot by three-foot square of darkness in the ceiling. Michael glares at you suspiciously. "I just got finished telling you," Michael says. "It's just a damn headache!" Michael cocks his head, as though listening to something. Michael stumbles off to the upstairs landing, muttering something about "needing some privacy . . ." Library Dark paneling and a deep, green carpet give this room the feel of some blue-blooded country squire's literary refuge. Books line the walls from floor to ceiling, and a leather armchair sits near the window. To the west, a pair of sliding doors opens onto the study, while the hall lies north. From here you can go north to the upstairs hall, or west to the study. They're already open. But you aren't in anything at the moment. Library Dark paneling and a deep, green carpet give this room the feel of some blue-blooded country squire's literary refuge. Books line the walls from floor to ceiling, and a leather armchair sits near the window. To the west, a pair of sliding doors opens onto the study, while the hall lies north. Upstairs Hall The shuttered window at the end of the hall throws a gloomy rectangle of light onto the bare wooden floor. Doorways lead north and south. A rickety ladder stands in the middle of the hall, descending from a three-foot by three-foot square of darkness in the ceiling. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Foyer A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north is the front door. Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer. Michael shuffles in from the back hall. He scowls at you when he sees you, as though he'd hoped he wouldn't find you here. You open the front door. Front of the House Gnarled and ancient trees surround a small clearing at the top of the hill. The road enters the clearing from the northwest and sweeps around in a wide curve, ending in front of the house. At the south end of the clearing stands the Verlac family mansion, its front door wide open. The hairs on the back of your neck prickle as you step outside. Something is wrong. Something is very wrong with the air. A heavy, charged sensation, like standing under high-tension power lines; a fleeting odor of spoiled meat drifting on the wind. You can't put your finger on it, but it might have something to do with that gigantic, swirling hole in the sky. The sky is an unbroken blanket of seething gray clouds in every direction, punctured by a massive, swirling hole. Visible even through the barren canopy of branches overhead, it hangs in the sky, ponderously turning like a vast, inverted whirlpool and sucking long, gray streamers of cloud up into itself. Scenic View Halfway up the hill, a break in the trees reveals a panoramic view of the Miskahawnee River Valley and the squalid little town of Anchorhead nestled within it. The road doubles back on itself, east to southeast, winding its tortuous way up to the top. Chilly Avenue It is noticeably cooler along this lonely street at the edge of town, perhaps due to some unwholesome vapour rising from the waters of the nearby Miskahawnee. Dirt roads lead west and south, into the dense woods at the edge of town. Riverwalk This street follows along the mud-slick banks of the Miskahawnee before bending south, turning into a misty avenue between the trees. Steep, stone steps lead down to the water's edge. The town square lies west, and a vacant lot lies east. Town Square The claustrophobic streets open out here, onto this wide expanse of uneven pavestones in the center of town. The municipal courthouse stands at the south end of the square. Avenues to the west and east lead back into the cramped, ingrown streets, while to the north lies Whateley Bridge. In the center of the square, rising from a circular lawn of unhealthy-looking grass, stands a stone obelisk. Riverwalk This street follows along the mud-slick banks of the Miskahawnee before bending south, turning into a misty avenue between the trees. Steep, stone steps lead down to the water's edge. The town square lies west, and a vacant lot lies east. A flurry of dead leaves tumbles past you in the wind. You follow the stone steps down to the edge of the river. Under the Bridge Whateley Bridge arches overhead, casting this little concrete ledge into deep shadow. At your feet, the dark waters lap quietly against the stones with a hollow, subterranean sound. Stone steps to the southeast lead back up to street level. An old manhole cover is set into the embankment under the bridge, closed and flush with the surrounding concrete. The cold wind blows harder, tugging at your clothes. You can't see any such thing. Neither of the keys you are carrying fits the lock. The lid is cast from solid iron, its edges dulled with rust. Unlike modern covers, this one is square, with hinges on one side. You can't see any such thing. You can't see any such thing. You can't see any such thing. You are wearing your wedding ring, and holding a broom, a keyring with a couple of keys on it, and a flashlight. You take the stairs back up to the street. Riverwalk This street follows along the mud-slick banks of the Miskahawnee before bending south, turning into a misty avenue between the trees. Steep, stone steps lead down to the water's edge. The town square lies west, and a vacant lot lies east. Town Square The claustrophobic streets open out here, onto this wide expanse of uneven pavestones in the center of town. The municipal courthouse stands at the south end of the square. Avenues to the west and east lead back into the cramped, ingrown streets, while to the north lies Whateley Bridge. In the center of the square, rising from a circular lawn of unhealthy-looking grass, stands a stone obelisk. In the distance, you can hear the lonesome keening of a train whistle drifting on the wind. Whateley Bridge A hoary monument of crumbling, moss-eaten flagstones, Whateley Bridge is possibly older than any other structure in the entire town. It spans the dark Miskahawnee River, connecting the north and south halves of the city and occasionally raining bits of mortar from its underside into the torpid water. North Square To the north, a gap in the crowded press of buildings opens onto a road heading out over a grassy heath. The main street continues to the south towards Whateley Bridge and the center of town, while a narrow side street leads east. At the top of a steep drive to the northwest, you can just make out the ivy-covered walls of the university. Streets lead east, south, and northwest, or you can leave town to the north. Whateley Bridge A hoary monument of crumbling, moss-eaten flagstones, Whateley Bridge is possibly older than any other structure in the entire town. It spans the dark Miskahawnee River, connecting the north and south halves of the city and occasionally raining bits of mortar from its underside into the torpid water. Town Square The claustrophobic streets open out here, onto this wide expanse of uneven pavestones in the center of town. The municipal courthouse stands at the south