mous four-poster bed, with a full-length dressing mirror standing opposite. An old-fashioned, accordion-style radiator gurgles quietly beneath the window. To the north a doorway leads back out to the hall, and a small bathroom lies east. You can see a bath towel, your trenchcoat, and a typewritten notice here. Taken. You put on the trenchcoat. You are wearing your trenchcoat and your wedding ring, and holding a broom, a keyring with a couple of keys on it, and a flashlight. The pockets of your trenchcoat contain a dog's skull, a thick copper key, your umbrella, an old-fashioned iron key, a silver locket, a newspaper, Ye Compleat Manuell of Wardes and Seales, and a scrapbook. Upstairs Landing A narrow hallway runs west, from the top of the stairs down the length of the house. To the south, directly opposite the stairs, is the master bedroom. Foyer A gloomy and forbidding entry hall, with tall, narrow doorways leading east, west, and south, and a steep staircase leading up to the second floor. To the north the front door stands wide open. Your luggage is still here, spread out all over the foyer. Front of the House Gnarled and ancient trees surround a small clearing at the top of the hill. The road enters the clearing from the northwest and sweeps around in a wide curve, ending in front of the house. At the south end of the clearing stands the Verlac family mansion, its front door wide open. Scenic View Halfway up the hill, a break in the trees reveals a panoramic view of the Miskahawnee River Valley and the squalid little town of Anchorhead nestled within it. The road doubles back on itself, east to southeast, winding its tortuous way up to the top. The wind moans fretfully. Chilly Avenue It is noticeably cooler along this lonely street at the edge of town, perhaps due to some unwholesome vapour rising from the waters of the nearby Miskahawnee. Dirt roads lead west and south, into the dense woods at the edge of town. Riverwalk This street follows along the mud-slick 