over with the thick copper key. The cold wind cuts through your clothes, chilling you to the bone. Woof — it's heavy! But you manage to get your fingers under the edge and pull the cover up with a groan, revealing a set of iron rungs leading down into darkness. The flashlight emits a warm yellow beam. You ease yourself down into the damp, smelly tunnel. Sewer Tunnel The air is damp, and moisture drips from the rusted pipes overhead. Foetid water flows sluggishly through a shallow channel running through the middle of the tunnel. A raised walkway to one side provides somewhat dryer footing, following the channel to the southeast and north. A set of iron rungs embedded in the wall leads up to an opening in the ceiling, letting in a column of dreary daylight. Vault This half-flooded chamber's crumbling brickwork must have been built much earlier than the rest of the tunnels. The ceiling is lost in darkness above you, but judging from the echoes it must be very high. Water trickling in from the tunnel to the northwest has pooled at the room's far end, which is submerged nearly waist-deep. Vault This half-flooded chamber's crumbling brickwork must have been built much earlier than the rest of the tunnels. The ceiling is lost in darkness above you, but judging from the echoes it must be very high. Water trickling in from the tunnel to the northwest has pooled at the room's far end, which is submerged nearly waist-deep. From here you can only go northwest. From here you can only go northwest. Sewer Tunnel The air is damp, and moisture drips from the rusted pipes overhead. Foetid water flows sluggishly through a shallow channel running through the middle of the tunnel. A raised walkway to one side provides somewhat dryer footing, following the channel to the southeast and north. A set of iron rungs embedded in the wall leads up to an opening in the ceiling, letting in a column of dreary daylight. The tunnel narrows and descends sharply as you go. Unless your sense of direction is mistaken, you must be crossing the Miskahawnee River — underneath it. Flooded Passage Knee-deep water floods this cramped tunnel, dripping constantly from the low ceiling and seeping through thousands of invisible fissures in the walls. There is barely enough room to stand up straight, much less stand somewhere dry. Higher ground lies to the south, while a metal grating blocks the way north. You notice something shiny fetched up against the base of the grating. It's an antique key, the kind with a long shaft and a big, square bit at the end. (slipping the thick copper key into your pocket to make room) Taken. (the old-fashioned brass key) It's an antique key, the kind with a long shaft and a big, square bit at the end. The grating is too firmly embedded in the concrete. Sewer Tunnel The air is damp, and moisture drips from the rusted pipes overhead. Foetid water flows sluggishly through a shallow channel running through the middle of the tunnel. A raised walkway to one side provides somewhat dryer footing, following the channel to the southeast and north. A set of iron rungs embedded in the wall leads up to an opening in the ceiling, letting in a column of dreary daylight. You can follow the tunnel to the southeast or to the north, or climb up through the manhole. You climb out through the opening. Under the Bridge Whateley Bridge arches overhead, casting this little concrete ledge into deep shadow. At your feet, the dark waters lap quietly against the stones with a hollow, subterranean sound. Stone steps to the southeast lead back up to street level. An old manhole cover is set into the embankment under the bridge, open to the darkness below.
